\"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"373-423 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"3-10 MPa, with substantial CO2 availability\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"CO2-rich water contacting freshly exposed Fe0; anoxic conditions. Explicit H+ represents the net proton inventory supplied through fluid and mineral buffering. Product recovery requires cooling and separation from a dilute mixed-organic inventory.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"A lumped surface-reaction network reduces inorganic carbon and forms carbon-carbon bonds while Fe0 is oxidized to Fe2+. Formation of one pyruvic-acid molecule from three CO2 molecules requires ten reducing equivalents; the elementary sequence is not fully resolved.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Native-iron experiments support formation of acetyl-CoA-pathway-related products, including pyruvate, from CO2 under hydrothermal laboratory conditions (Varma et al., 2018). This does not establish a selective, high-flux natural pyruvate source. Native iron availability, passivation, competing hydrogen evolution and low product concentrations are major limitations. Fe2+ abundance alone cannot substitute for the consumed Fe0.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Candidate methanethiol-carbon monoxide carbonylation to pyruvate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_015\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Iron-sulfide surfaces and transient iron-carbonyl/sulfur complexes as proposed promoters; catalytic turnover is not established\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"Approximately 523 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 200 MPa in the motivating laboratory analogue\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic, CO-rich, sulfur-bearing pressurized water. This pressure greatly exceeds ordinary seafloor vent pressures. Rapid cooling is required before subsequent amination, and the stated net feedstock conversion remains unverified.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Proposed carbonylative carbon-carbon assembly combines a thiol-derived methyl group with two CO-derived carbons, followed by hydrolysis and sulfur release. The equation is an atom-balanced candidate net transformation, not a resolved catalytic cycle.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative extrapolation. Carbonylated iron-sulfur chemistry and pyruvate formation under severe experimental hydrothermal conditions motivate the branch (Cody et al., 2000), but that evidence does not establish this exact clean methanethiol-to-pyruvate conversion. CO activation on iron-sulfur systems gives this alternative chemical motivation, while pressure requirements, catalyst speciation, competing C2 products and pyruvate destruction make it substantially less secure than a demonstrated preparative route.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrogen-driven reductive amination of pyruvate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_017\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Ni-bearing native-metal surface, such as an awaruite-like Fe-Ni alloy, proposed for H2 activation\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-400 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"5-30 MPa total pressure with appreciable H2 fugacity\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Accessible metallic Ni sites on a proposed Fe-Ni surface\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Approximate pH 8-10 in a cooler H2-rich mixing zone; ammonia enrichment is required. Bulk oceanic ammonia concentrations are not assumed sufficient.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Pyruvate and ammonia form an imine or iminium intermediate. Surface-activated hydrogen reduces its carbon-nitrogen double bond, producing equal-access L and D configurations.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Reductive amination is established chemistry, but the specified natural-alloy implementation is a candidate requiring experimental calibration. Hydrothermal pyruvate chemistry is strongly condition-dependent (Novikov and Copley, 2013). Direct carbonyl reduction to lactate, other pyruvate reactions, sulfur poisoning of metallic sites and insufficient ammonia can suppress alanine formation. H2 abundance is useful only if an effective activation surface persists.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Native-iron-driven reductive amination of pyruvate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_018\", \"mol_019\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_020\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Native iron, mol_018, consumed as a two-electron reductant per alanine molecule\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-400 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"5-20 MPa\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Buffered mixing region, approximately pH 6.5-9; freshly exposed Fe0 and locally concentrated total ammonia. H+ denotes net proton delivery by the buffer, not a separate strongly acidic requirement.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia-carbonyl condensation is coupled to reduction by oxidation of Fe0 to Fe2+. Net reduction of the imine requires two electrons per alanine molecule, with water released.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is a candidate geochemical implementation of metal-driven reductive amination, not an assertion of a validated yield at every listed condition. It differs materially from rxn_008 because iron is consumed and molecular H2 activation is not required. Native-metal scarcity, passivation, hydrogen evolution and imine availability are bottlenecks. No biological enzyme, pyridoxal cofactor or stereoselective surface is assumed.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Pyruvate trapping as an oxime\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_021\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_022\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"5-20 MPa\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Cool hydrothermal mixing interface; approximate pH 5-7. Hydroxylamine must encounter pyruvate before substantial decomposition or unproductive reduction. E/Z oxime geometry is not resolved.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydroxylamine adds to the keto carbonyl and loses water to form the pyruvate oxime. This captures nitrogen through a different intermediate than ammonia-based imine formation.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Hydroxylamine/iron chemistry has been used to connect keto acids with amino acids in experimental protometabolic networks (Muchowska et al., 2019). Oxime formation is chemically well grounded, but a sustained geological hydroxylamine supply is not established. Hydroxylamine is therefore an explicitly conditional starting feedstock, not an assumed abundant constituent of anoxic seawater. The chosen hydrothermal window is a candidate setting rather than a reported universal optimum.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Catalytic hydrogenation and deoxygenation of pyruvate oxime\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_022\", \"mol_017\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Ni-bearing native-metal surface proposed to activate H2 and promote oxime reduction\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-400 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"5-30 MPa total pressure with appreciable H2 fugacity\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Accessible metallic Ni sites on a proposed Fe-Ni surface\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic, approximately pH 6-9; freshly active metallic surface. Sulfide poisoning and competing oxime cleavage must be assessed.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrogenation of the carbon-nitrogen functionality and hydrogenolysis of the nitrogen-oxygen bond give alanine and water. The net conversion requires four reducing equivalents per oxime, supplied by molecular hydrogen.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Oxime-to-amine hydrogenation is established synthetic chemistry, but efficient operation on an unmodified geological Fe-Ni surface under this aqueous window is speculative. It is retained as a chemically motivated H2 alternative to consumed-metal reduction, not as an experimentally proven vent pathway. Incomplete reduction, loss of nitrogen and catalyst poisoning are possible. Neither E/Z geometry nor an achiral metal surface supplies L-selectivity.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Native-iron reduction of pyruvate oxime\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_022\", \"mol_018\", \"mol_019\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_020\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Native iron, mol_018, consumed as the electron donor\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-390 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"5-20 MPa\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Mildly acidic to near-neutral buffered interface, approximately pH 4-7; freshly reactive Fe0. Proton supply and surface renewal must offset consumption and passivation.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Electron transfer from Fe0, protonation, carbon-nitrogen reduction and nitrogen-oxygen bond cleavage convert the oxime to alanine. Four electrons are required per oxime, corresponding to oxidation of two Fe atoms to Fe2+.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Hydroxylamine/metallic-iron amino-acid synthesis provides experimental motivation for this branch (Muchowska et al., 2019). The separately represented oxime is a mechanistic intermediate; the graph does not claim that the cited work demonstrated every isolated step under the listed geological conditions. Hydroxylamine supply, metallic iron persistence and competing reactions remain the principal environmental gaps. The product is racemic.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct Strecker hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Acetaldehyde, ammonia and HCN form racemic alaninonitrile, followed by aqueous nitrile hydrolysis. The S intermediate supplies L-alanine; D-alanine is formed in parallel. Hot-fluid transfer and hydrolysis are the main environmental bottlenecks.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_006\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Strecker entry through a hydantoin reservoir\",\n      \"description\": \"The aminonitrile captures inorganic carbon to form 5-methylhydantoin before hydrolytic release of alanine. This introduces a distinct cyclic intermediate and an additional cleavage bottleneck; it does not produce an L excess.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_008\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"CO2-derived pyruvate with ammonia and hydrogen\",\n      \"description\": \"Native-iron-mediated CO2 chemistry supplies pyruvate, followed by ammonia incorporation and candidate mineral-catalyzed hydrogenation. This combines an experimentally motivated carbon-source module with an uncertain natural H2-activation implementation.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_006\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_016\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"CO2-derived pyruvate with ammonia and consumed iron\",\n      \"description\": \"CO2 reduction supplies pyruvate, which undergoes candidate reductive amination using additional Fe0 as a consumed electron donor. Iron demand, surface passivation and ammonia concentration constrain the route.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_006\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_016\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"CO2-derived pyruvate through oxime hydrogenation\",\n      \"description\": \"Pyruvate obtained from CO2 is trapped with hydroxylamine, then reduced using H2 on a proposed native-metal surface. Geological hydroxylamine supply and oxime hydrogenation on natural alloys are unresolved.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_006\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_022\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"CO2-derived pyruvate through iron-reduced oxime\",\n      \"description\": \"Native-iron carbon fixation is followed by oxime formation and consumed-iron reduction. The component chemistries have experimental motivation, but their integrated operation and natural hydroxylamine supply have not been established.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_006\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_022\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Carbonylative pyruvate with ammonia and hydrogen\",\n      \"description\": \"A speculative methanethiol/CO carbonylation module supplies pyruvate for ammonia/H2 reductive amination. The entire sequence remains conditional on validating the proposed carbon-source reaction and the mineral hydrogenation catalyst.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_016\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Carbonylative pyruvate with ammonia and consumed iron\",\n      \"description\": \"Candidate carbonylative pyruvate synthesis is followed by Fe0-driven ammonia incorporation. The high-pressure carbon-source module and the efficiency of direct reductive amination are separate unresolved bottlenecks.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_007\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_016\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Carbonylative pyruvate through oxime hydrogenation\",\n      \"description\": \"The speculative CO/thiol module feeds oxime formation and proposed H2-driven oxime reduction after cooling. Carbonylation selectivity, hydroxylamine supply and natural-metal catalytic activity all require validation.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_007\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_022\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"Carbonylative pyruvate through iron-reduced oxime\",\n      \"description\": \"Candidate carbonylative pyruvate formation is followed by hydroxylamine trapping and iron reduction. The downstream chemistry is experimentally motivated, but it does not validate the speculative upstream carbonylation or establish an integrated geological pathway.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_007\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_022\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\n    \"mol_001\",\n    \"mol_002\",\n    \"mol_006\",\n    \"mol_007\",\n    \"mol_010\",\n    \"mol_011\",\n    \"mol_012\",\n    \"mol_016\",\n    \"mol_017\",\n    \"mol_018\",\n    \"mol_019\",\n    \"mol_020\",\n    \"mol_022\"\n  ],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_016\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_011\",\n        \"rxn_012\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_011\",\n        \"rxn_012\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_002\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_005\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_010\",\n        \"rxn_011\",\n        \"rxn_012\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_007\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_020\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_006\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    }\n  ]\n}", "parsed_response": {"target_molecule": "L-Alanine", "overall_strategy": "Construct two cyanide-based pathways and eight pyruvate-based pathways. The latter combine two carbon-source modules with two nitrogen-entry chemistries and two reductants. These are ten distinct reaction sequences, not ten independently demonstrated prebiotic syntheses; uncertain mineral implementations and precursor supplies are identified below. All stereogenic reactions use achiral reagents and therefore produce L-alanine together with D-alanine. The target is the L component of a racemate, not isolated enantiopure material; no unsupported prebiotic L-selective resolution is assumed. The graph contains 22 species and 12 net reactions, retaining explicit redox reagents and relevant enantiomers. Paired enantiomer transformations within one reaction node summarize parallel chemistry, not reactions between the enantiomers. Hydrolysis and carbon-fixation nodes are explicitly lumped transformations. Acid/base compounds are represented in one conventional form, with solution speciation governed by the stated pH. Carbon dioxide is both an externally supplied feedstock and a recycled product. Surface-to-hydrothermal transfer is required only where hotter aqueous hydrolysis is invoked, and its dilution and preservation problems remain unresolved.", "molecules": [{"id": "mol_001", "formula": "H2O", "iupac_name": "oxidane", "common_name": "Water", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_002", "formula": "NH3", "iupac_name": "azane", "common_name": "Ammonia", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_003", "formula": "HCN", "iupac_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "common_name": "Hydrogen cyanide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_004", "formula": "C2H4O", "iupac_name": "ethanal", "common_name": "Acetaldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H4O/c1-2-3/h2H,1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_005", "formula": "C2H5N", "iupac_name": "ethanimine", "common_name": "Acetaldehyde imine", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_006", "formula": "C3H6N2", "iupac_name": "(2S)-2-aminopropanenitrile", "common_name": "(S)-Alaninonitrile", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_007", "formula": "C3H6N2", "iupac_name": "(2R)-2-aminopropanenitrile", "common_name": "(R)-Alaninonitrile", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_008", "formula": "C4H6N2O2", "iupac_name": "(5S)-5-methylimidazolidine-2,4-dione", "common_name": "(S)-5-Methylhydantoin", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_009", "formula": "C4H6N2O2", "iupac_name": "(5R)-5-methylimidazolidine-2,4-dione", "common_name": "(R)-5-Methylhydantoin", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_010", "formula": "C3H7NO2", "iupac_name": "(2S)-2-aminopropanoic acid", "common_name": "L-Alanine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H7NO2/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h2H,4H2,1H3,(H,5,6)/t2-/m0/s1", "role": "target", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_011", "formula": "C3H7NO2", "iupac_name": "(2R)-2-aminopropanoic acid", "common_name": "D-Alanine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H7NO2/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h2H,4H2,1H3,(H,5,6)/t2-/m1/s1", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_012", "formula": "CO2", "iupac_name": "carbon dioxide", "common_name": "Carbon dioxide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CO2/c2-1-3", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_013", "formula": "CO", "iupac_name": "carbon monoxide", "common_name": "Carbon monoxide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CO/c1-2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_014", "formula": "CH4S", "iupac_name": "methanethiol", "common_name": "Methyl mercaptan", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH4S/c1-2/h2H,1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_015", "formula": "H2S", "iupac_name": "sulfane", "common_name": "Hydrogen sulfide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2S/h1H2", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_016", "formula": "C3H4O3", "iupac_name": "2-oxopropanoic acid", "common_name": "Pyruvic acid", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H4O3/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h1H3,(H,5,6)", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_017", "formula": "H2", "iupac_name": "hydrogen", "common_name": "Molecular hydrogen", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_018", "formula": "Fe", "iupac_name": "iron", "common_name": "Native iron", "inchi": "InChI=1S/Fe", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_019", "formula": "H+", "iupac_name": "hydron", "common_name": "Proton", "inchi": "InChI=1S/p+1", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_020", "formula": "Fe2+", "iupac_name": "iron(2+)", "common_name": "Ferrous ion", "inchi": "InChI=1S/Fe/q+2", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_021", "formula": "NH3O", "iupac_name": "hydroxylamine", "common_name": "Hydroxylamine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3NO/c1-2/h2H,1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_022", "formula": "C3H5NO3", "iupac_name": "2-(hydroxyimino)propanoic acid", "common_name": "Pyruvic acid oxime, unspecified E/Z geometry", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}], "reactions": [{"id": "rxn_001", "name": "Acetaldehyde-ammonia imine equilibration", "inputs": ["mol_004", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_005", "mol_001"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic, concentrated evaporitic solution; approximate pH 8-9; partial evaporation rather than complete desiccation. Acetaldehyde and ammonia require replenishment because both are volatile."}, "mechanism": "Ammonia adds to the aldehyde carbonyl, followed by carbinolamine dehydration. Ethanimine is a transient equilibrium species and is trapped by cyanide in rxn_002 rather than assumed to accumulate as an isolated compound.", "reasoning": "This is a mechanistic subdivision of established Strecker chemistry (Strecker, 1850). No mineral catalyst is required. Imine hydrolysis, aldehyde loss and competing carbonyl chemistry limit accumulation. Evaporation can increase encounter rates, but strong surface UV is not assumed to improve this reaction and may instead destroy reactants."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Nonselective cyanide addition to ethanimine", "inputs": ["mol_005", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_006", "mol_007"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Approximate pH 8-9, providing some nucleophilic cyanide while retaining dissolved HCN; continuous coupling to imine formation. Mineral turbidity or shallow burial may reduce destructive UV exposure."}, "mechanism": "Cyanide attacks the planar imine or iminium carbon, followed by proton transfer. Attack on either face gives the S and R aminonitriles without a prescribed enantiomeric excess.", "reasoning": "The carbon skeleton and nitrogen incorporation follow classical alanine Strecker synthesis (Strecker, 1850). HCN supply, volatilization and side reactions remain geochemical bottlenecks. The two outputs explicitly represent racemic formation; producing only the S aminonitrile under these achiral conditions would be unjustified."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Alaninonitrile hydrolysis to racemic alanine", "inputs": ["mol_006", "mol_007", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_011", "mol_002"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "370-420 K", "pressure": "5-30 MPa, maintaining liquid water", "catalyst": "Hydronium supplied by acidic fluid", "aqueous": true, "other": "Acidic mixing fluid, approximately pH 1-3 when referenced to room-temperature measurement; residence time must be established experimentally. Surface-derived aminonitriles must survive transport and dilution. Cool the products after hydrolysis."}, "mechanism": "Acid-assisted nitrile hydration gives an alaninamide stage, followed by amide hydrolysis. The net reaction consumes water and releases ammonia, predominantly ammonium at the stated pH. S aminonitrile gives L-alanine and R aminonitrile gives D-alanine if configuration is retained.", "reasoning": "Aminonitrile hydrolysis is the established completion of the Strecker route (Strecker, 1850). This node is a net transformation, not a claim that the amide stage is absent. Geochemical acidity, residence time and transport are less controlled than laboratory hydrolysis. Additional racemization during heating would not create an L excess."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Carbon dioxide capture and hydantoin formation", "inputs": ["mol_006", "mol_007", "mol_012"], "outputs": ["mol_008", "mol_009"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "320-360 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Concentrated, ammonia-rich carbonate solution; approximate pH 8-10. The CO2 node represents the dissolved inorganic-carbon reservoir after acid/base equilibration."}, "mechanism": "Aminonitrile carbamylation by inorganic carbon is followed by cyclization involving the nitrile and rearrangement to the hydantoin. The net transformation incorporates one additional carbon per hydantoin without net water consumption.", "reasoning": "This branch follows the aminonitrile-based interpretation of Bucherer-Bergs hydantoin synthesis (Bucherer and Steiner, 1934). Hydantoin formation offers a chemically distinct storage and processing route rather than a second label for direct nitrile hydrolysis. Concentrated ammonium carbonate conditions are a requirement, not an assumed property of all surface pools. Achiral processing preserves an overall racemic inventory."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Hydantoin ring opening and decarbamylation", "inputs": ["mol_008", "mol_009", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_011", "mol_002", "mol_012"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "410-470 K", "pressure": "5-30 MPa, maintaining liquid water", "catalyst": "Hydronium supplied by acidic fluid", "aqueous": true, "other": "Acidic hydrothermal mixing zone, approximately pH 1-3 on a room-temperature reference basis; prolonged exposure may be necessary. Transfer, dilution, incomplete cleavage and amino-acid decomposition must be evaluated."}, "mechanism": "Hydrolytic ring opening produces an N-carbamoylalanine stage. Further hydrolysis and decarbamylation release alanine, ammonia and carbon dioxide. The node summarizes both stages.", "reasoning": "Hydantoin conversion to amino acids is established laboratory chemistry associated with the Bucherer-Bergs route. However, efficient cleavage commonly uses more forcing acid or base conditions than ordinary natural waters provide. Conversion rate and alanine survival in the stated geological window are therefore unresolved bottlenecks, not demonstrated high-yield outcomes. No asymmetric catalyst or hydantoinase enzyme is invoked."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Native-iron-driven carbon dioxide reduction and C3 assembly", "inputs": ["mol_012", "mol_018", "mol_019"], "outputs": ["mol_016", "mol_020", "mol_001"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Native iron, mol_018, serving as a consumed reductant and reactive surface rather than a regenerated catalyst"], "conditions": {"temperature": "373-423 K", "pressure": "3-10 MPa, with substantial CO2 availability", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "CO2-rich water contacting freshly exposed Fe0; anoxic conditions. Explicit H+ represents the net proton inventory supplied through fluid and mineral buffering. Product recovery requires cooling and separation from a dilute mixed-organic inventory."}, "mechanism": "A lumped surface-reaction network reduces inorganic carbon and forms carbon-carbon bonds while Fe0 is oxidized to Fe2+. Formation of one pyruvic-acid molecule from three CO2 molecules requires ten reducing equivalents; the elementary sequence is not fully resolved.", "reasoning": "Native-iron experiments support formation of acetyl-CoA-pathway-related products, including pyruvate, from CO2 under hydrothermal laboratory conditions (Varma et al., 2018). This does not establish a selective, high-flux natural pyruvate source. Native iron availability, passivation, competing hydrogen evolution and low product concentrations are major limitations. Fe2+ abundance alone cannot substitute for the consumed Fe0."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Candidate methanethiol-carbon monoxide carbonylation to pyruvate", "inputs": ["mol_014", "mol_013", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_016", "mol_015"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Iron-sulfide surfaces and transient iron-carbonyl/sulfur complexes as proposed promoters; catalytic turnover is not established"], "conditions": {"temperature": "Approximately 523 K", "pressure": "Approximately 200 MPa in the motivating laboratory analogue", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic, CO-rich, sulfur-bearing pressurized water. This pressure greatly exceeds ordinary seafloor vent pressures. Rapid cooling is required before subsequent amination, and the stated net feedstock conversion remains unverified."}, "mechanism": "Proposed carbonylative carbon-carbon assembly combines a thiol-derived methyl group with two CO-derived carbons, followed by hydrolysis and sulfur release. The equation is an atom-balanced candidate net transformation, not a resolved catalytic cycle.", "reasoning": "Speculative extrapolation. Carbonylated iron-sulfur chemistry and pyruvate formation under severe experimental hydrothermal conditions motivate the branch (Cody et al., 2000), but that evidence does not establish this exact clean methanethiol-to-pyruvate conversion. CO activation on iron-sulfur systems gives this alternative chemical motivation, while pressure requirements, catalyst speciation, competing C2 products and pyruvate destruction make it substantially less secure than a demonstrated preparative route."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Hydrogen-driven reductive amination of pyruvate", "inputs": ["mol_016", "mol_002", "mol_017"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_011", "mol_001"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Ni-bearing native-metal surface, such as an awaruite-like Fe-Ni alloy, proposed for H2 activation"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-400 K", "pressure": "5-30 MPa total pressure with appreciable H2 fugacity", "catalyst": "Accessible metallic Ni sites on a proposed Fe-Ni surface", "aqueous": true, "other": "Approximate pH 8-10 in a cooler H2-rich mixing zone; ammonia enrichment is required. Bulk oceanic ammonia concentrations are not assumed sufficient."}, "mechanism": "Pyruvate and ammonia form an imine or iminium intermediate. Surface-activated hydrogen reduces its carbon-nitrogen double bond, producing equal-access L and D configurations.", "reasoning": "Reductive amination is established chemistry, but the specified natural-alloy implementation is a candidate requiring experimental calibration. Hydrothermal pyruvate chemistry is strongly condition-dependent (Novikov and Copley, 2013). Direct carbonyl reduction to lactate, other pyruvate reactions, sulfur poisoning of metallic sites and insufficient ammonia can suppress alanine formation. H2 abundance is useful only if an effective activation surface persists."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Native-iron-driven reductive amination of pyruvate", "inputs": ["mol_016", "mol_002", "mol_018", "mol_019"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_011", "mol_020", "mol_001"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Native iron, mol_018, consumed as a two-electron reductant per alanine molecule"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-400 K", "pressure": "5-20 MPa", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Buffered mixing region, approximately pH 6.5-9; freshly exposed Fe0 and locally concentrated total ammonia. H+ denotes net proton delivery by the buffer, not a separate strongly acidic requirement."}, "mechanism": "Ammonia-carbonyl condensation is coupled to reduction by oxidation of Fe0 to Fe2+. Net reduction of the imine requires two electrons per alanine molecule, with water released.", "reasoning": "This is a candidate geochemical implementation of metal-driven reductive amination, not an assertion of a validated yield at every listed condition. It differs materially from rxn_008 because iron is consumed and molecular H2 activation is not required. Native-metal scarcity, passivation, hydrogen evolution and imine availability are bottlenecks. No biological enzyme, pyridoxal cofactor or stereoselective surface is assumed."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Pyruvate trapping as an oxime", "inputs": ["mol_016", "mol_021"], "outputs": ["mol_022", "mol_001"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "350-370 K", "pressure": "5-20 MPa", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Cool hydrothermal mixing interface; approximate pH 5-7. Hydroxylamine must encounter pyruvate before substantial decomposition or unproductive reduction. E/Z oxime geometry is not resolved."}, "mechanism": "Hydroxylamine adds to the keto carbonyl and loses water to form the pyruvate oxime. This captures nitrogen through a different intermediate than ammonia-based imine formation.", "reasoning": "Hydroxylamine/iron chemistry has been used to connect keto acids with amino acids in experimental protometabolic networks (Muchowska et al., 2019). Oxime formation is chemically well grounded, but a sustained geological hydroxylamine supply is not established. Hydroxylamine is therefore an explicitly conditional starting feedstock, not an assumed abundant constituent of anoxic seawater. The chosen hydrothermal window is a candidate setting rather than a reported universal optimum."}, {"id": "rxn_011", "name": "Catalytic hydrogenation and deoxygenation of pyruvate oxime", "inputs": ["mol_022", "mol_017"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_011", "mol_001"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Ni-bearing native-metal surface proposed to activate H2 and promote oxime reduction"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-400 K", "pressure": "5-30 MPa total pressure with appreciable H2 fugacity", "catalyst": "Accessible metallic Ni sites on a proposed Fe-Ni surface", "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic, approximately pH 6-9; freshly active metallic surface. Sulfide poisoning and competing oxime cleavage must be assessed."}, "mechanism": "Hydrogenation of the carbon-nitrogen functionality and hydrogenolysis of the nitrogen-oxygen bond give alanine and water. The net conversion requires four reducing equivalents per oxime, supplied by molecular hydrogen.", "reasoning": "Oxime-to-amine hydrogenation is established synthetic chemistry, but efficient operation on an unmodified geological Fe-Ni surface under this aqueous window is speculative. It is retained as a chemically motivated H2 alternative to consumed-metal reduction, not as an experimentally proven vent pathway. Incomplete reduction, loss of nitrogen and catalyst poisoning are possible. Neither E/Z geometry nor an achiral metal surface supplies L-selectivity."}, {"id": "rxn_012", "name": "Native-iron reduction of pyruvate oxime", "inputs": ["mol_022", "mol_018", "mol_019"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_011", "mol_020", "mol_001"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Native iron, mol_018, consumed as the electron donor"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-390 K", "pressure": "5-20 MPa", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Mildly acidic to near-neutral buffered interface, approximately pH 4-7; freshly reactive Fe0. Proton supply and surface renewal must offset consumption and passivation."}, "mechanism": "Electron transfer from Fe0, protonation, carbon-nitrogen reduction and nitrogen-oxygen bond cleavage convert the oxime to alanine. Four electrons are required per oxime, corresponding to oxidation of two Fe atoms to Fe2+.", "reasoning": "Hydroxylamine/metallic-iron amino-acid synthesis provides experimental motivation for this branch (Muchowska et al., 2019). The separately represented oxime is a mechanistic intermediate; the graph does not claim that the cited work demonstrated every isolated step under the listed geological conditions. Hydroxylamine supply, metallic iron persistence and competing reactions remain the principal environmental gaps. The product is racemic."}], "pathways": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "name": "Direct Strecker hydrolysis", "description": "Acetaldehyde, ammonia and HCN form racemic alaninonitrile, followed by aqueous nitrile hydrolysis. The S intermediate supplies L-alanine; D-alanine is formed in parallel. Hot-fluid transfer and hydrolysis are the main environmental bottlenecks.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_005", "mol_006"]}, {"pathway_id": "P2", "name": "Strecker entry through a hydantoin reservoir", "description": "The aminonitrile captures inorganic carbon to form 5-methylhydantoin before hydrolytic release of alanine. This introduces a distinct cyclic intermediate and an additional cleavage bottleneck; it does not produce an L excess.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_004", "rxn_005"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_005", "mol_006", "mol_008"]}, {"pathway_id": "P3", "name": "CO2-derived pyruvate with ammonia and hydrogen", "description": "Native-iron-mediated CO2 chemistry supplies pyruvate, followed by ammonia incorporation and candidate mineral-catalyzed hydrogenation. This combines an experimentally motivated carbon-source module with an uncertain natural H2-activation implementation.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_006", "rxn_008"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_016"]}, {"pathway_id": "P4", "name": "CO2-derived pyruvate with ammonia and consumed iron", "description": "CO2 reduction supplies pyruvate, which undergoes candidate reductive amination using additional Fe0 as a consumed electron donor. Iron demand, surface passivation and ammonia concentration constrain the route.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_006", "rxn_009"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_016"]}, {"pathway_id": "P5", "name": "CO2-derived pyruvate through oxime hydrogenation", "description": "Pyruvate obtained from CO2 is trapped with hydroxylamine, then reduced using H2 on a proposed native-metal surface. Geological hydroxylamine supply and oxime hydrogenation on natural alloys are unresolved.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_006", "rxn_010", "rxn_011"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_016", "mol_022"]}, {"pathway_id": "P6", "name": "CO2-derived pyruvate through iron-reduced oxime", "description": "Native-iron carbon fixation is followed by oxime formation and consumed-iron reduction. The component chemistries have experimental motivation, but their integrated operation and natural hydroxylamine supply have not been established.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_006", "rxn_010", "rxn_012"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_016", "mol_022"]}, {"pathway_id": "P7", "name": "Carbonylative pyruvate with ammonia and hydrogen", "description": "A speculative methanethiol/CO carbonylation module supplies pyruvate for ammonia/H2 reductive amination. The entire sequence remains conditional on validating the proposed carbon-source reaction and the mineral hydrogenation catalyst.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_007", "rxn_008"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_016"]}, {"pathway_id": "P8", "name": "Carbonylative pyruvate with ammonia and consumed iron", "description": "Candidate carbonylative pyruvate synthesis is followed by Fe0-driven ammonia incorporation. The high-pressure carbon-source module and the efficiency of direct reductive amination are separate unresolved bottlenecks.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_007", "rxn_009"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_016"]}, {"pathway_id": "P9", "name": "Carbonylative pyruvate through oxime hydrogenation", "description": "The speculative CO/thiol module feeds oxime formation and proposed H2-driven oxime reduction after cooling. Carbonylation selectivity, hydroxylamine supply and natural-metal catalytic activity all require validation.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_007", "rxn_010", "rxn_011"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_016", "mol_022"]}, {"pathway_id": "P10", "name": "Carbonylative pyruvate through iron-reduced oxime", "description": "Candidate carbonylative pyruvate formation is followed by hydroxylamine trapping and iron reduction. The downstream chemistry is experimentally motivated, but it does not validate the speculative upstream carbonylation or establish an integrated geological pathway.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_007", "rxn_010", "rxn_012"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_016", "mol_022"]}], "hub_molecules": ["mol_001", "mol_002", "mol_006", "mol_007", "mol_010", "mol_011", "mol_012", "mol_016", "mol_017", "mol_018", "mol_019", "mol_020", "mol_022"], "convergence_points": [{"molecule_id": "mol_016", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_006", "rxn_007"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_008", "rxn_009", "rxn_010"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_010", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_003", "rxn_005", "rxn_008", "rxn_009", "rxn_011", "rxn_012"], "outgoing_reactions": []}, {"molecule_id": "mol_011", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_003", "rxn_005", "rxn_008", "rxn_009", "rxn_011", "rxn_012"], "outgoing_reactions": []}, {"molecule_id": "mol_002", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_003", "rxn_005"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_001", "rxn_008", "rxn_009"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_001", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_001", "rxn_006", "rxn_008", "rxn_009", "rxn_010", "rxn_011", "rxn_012"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_003", "rxn_005", "rxn_007"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_020", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_006", "rxn_009", "rxn_012"], "outgoing_reactions": []}]}, "reasoning": "", "duration_ms": 610569.6604251862, "success": true, "error_message": null}
{"timestamp": "2026-10-19T04:36:55.059905", "agent_name": "CriticAgent", "method_name": "evaluate_network", "step": null, "attempt": null, "system_message": "You are evaluating a COMPLETE SYNTHESIS NETWORK, not individual reactions.\n\n## Neutrality\n- Do not reward or penalize a network for using (or not using) any particular feedstock, intermediate,\n  reaction family, or prebiotic framework. Judge only the evidence and the chemistry.\n- The number of shared/hub molecules, the number of convergence points, and the order or number of\n  environments used are NOT scoring criteria.\n\n## Evaluation Criteria\n\n### 1. Network Validity (0.0-1.0)\n- Are there sufficient molecule and reaction nodes?\n- Is the network connected (no isolated subgraphs)?\n- Are molecule IDs used consistently, and are reactions approximately balanced?\n- When a DETERMINISTIC ATOM/CHARGE BALANCE CHECK section is provided, treat it as ground truth for\n  mass and charge conservation; reactions it reports as UNBALANCED are conservation violations.\n\n### 2. Chemical Feasibility (0.0-1.0)\n- Is each reaction chemically sound?\n- Are the conditions appropriate for each environment?\n- Are the mechanisms plausible?\n\n### 3. Environmental Consistency (0.0-1.0)\n- Are each reaction's conditions (temperature, pressure, pH, catalysts, energy source) consistent with its stated environment?\n- If molecules are transferred between environments, is the transfer physically justified?\n- If a catalyst or mineral agent is cited, is it realistic and present in that environment?\n\n### 4. Pathway Coherence (0.0-1.0)\n- Do pathways form valid routes to the target?\n- Are key intermediates properly identified?\n- Is the overall strategy sound?\n\n### 5. Target Achievement (0.0-1.0)\n- Does the network actually produce the target molecule?\n- Are the final steps chemically valid?\n\n### 6. Literature Alignment (0.0-1.0) - When research findings are provided\n- Are reactions supported by experimental evidence from cited studies?\n- Are literature citations present in reaction reasoning?\n- Does the network acknowledge critiques and known challenges identified in the research?\n- Departures from the research are acceptable when explicitly and soundly justified.\n\n## Scoring Guidelines\n- 0.9-1.0: Excellent network, valid and chemically sound\n- 0.8-0.9: Good network with minor issues\n- 0.7-0.8: Acceptable but with notable concerns\n- 0.5-0.7: Significant problems with validity or chemistry\n- 0.0-0.5: Major issues, network is not viable\n\nA network is APPROVED if overall_confidence >= 0.9\n\nOutput your evaluation as JSON matching the NetworkEvaluation schema.\n", "user_message": "Evaluate this synthesis network:\n\nTARGET MOLECULE: L-Alanine\nOVERALL STRATEGY: Construct two cyanide-based pathways and eight pyruvate-based pathways. The latter combine two carbon-source modules with two nitrogen-entry chemistries and two reductants. These are ten distinct reaction sequences, not ten independently demonstrated prebiotic syntheses; uncertain mineral implementations and precursor supplies are identified below. All stereogenic reactions use achiral reagents and therefore produce L-alanine together with D-alanine. The target is the L component of a racemate, not isolated enantiopure material; no unsupported prebiotic L-selective resolution is assumed. The graph contains 22 species and 12 net reactions, retaining explicit redox reagents and relevant enantiomers. Paired enantiomer transformations within one reaction node summarize parallel chemistry, not reactions between the enantiomers. Hydrolysis and carbon-fixation nodes are explicitly lumped transformations. Acid/base compounds are represented in one conventional form, with solution speciation governed by the stated pH. Carbon dioxide is both an externally supplied feedstock and a recycled product. Surface-to-hydrothermal transfer is required only where hotter aqueous hydrolysis is invoked, and its dilution and preservation problems remain unresolved.\n\nMOLECULES (22 nodes):\n  - mol_001: H2O (Water) [starting]\n  - mol_002: NH3 (Ammonia) [starting]\n  - mol_003: HCN (Hydrogen cyanide) [starting]\n  - mol_004: C2H4O (Acetaldehyde) [starting]\n  - mol_005: C2H5N (Acetaldehyde imine) [intermediate]\n  - mol_006: C3H6N2 ((S)-Alaninonitrile) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_007: C3H6N2 ((R)-Alaninonitrile) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_008: C4H6N2O2 ((S)-5-Methylhydantoin) [intermediate]\n  - mol_009: C4H6N2O2 ((R)-5-Methylhydantoin) [intermediate]\n  - mol_010: C3H7NO2 (L-Alanine) [target]\n  - mol_011: C3H7NO2 (D-Alanine) [intermediate]\n  - mol_012: CO2 (Carbon dioxide) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_013: CO (Carbon monoxide) [starting]\n  - mol_014: CH4S (Methyl mercaptan) [starting]\n  - mol_015: H2S (Hydrogen sulfide) [intermediate]\n  - mol_016: C3H4O3 (Pyruvic acid) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_017: H2 (Molecular hydrogen) [starting]\n  - mol_018: Fe (Native iron) [starting]\n  - mol_019: H+ (Proton) [starting]\n  - mol_020: Fe2+ (Ferrous ion) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_021: NH3O (Hydroxylamine) [starting]\n  - mol_022: C3H5NO3 (Pyruvic acid oxime, unspecified E/Z geometry) [hub_intermediate]\n\nREACTIONS (12 nodes):\n  - rxn_001: mol_004, mol_002 -> mol_005, mol_001 (Acetaldehyde-ammonia imine equilibration) [Surface]\n  - rxn_002: mol_005, mol_003 -> mol_006, mol_007 (Nonselective cyanide addition to ethanimine) [Surface]\n  - rxn_003: mol_006, mol_007, mol_001 -> mol_010, mol_011, mol_002 (Alaninonitrile hydrolysis to racemic alanine) [Hydrothermal]\n  - rxn_004: mol_006, mol_007, mol_012 -> mol_008, mol_009 (Carbon dioxide capture and hydantoin formation) [Surface]\n  - rxn_005: mol_008, mol_009, mol_001 -> mol_010, mol_011, mol_002, mol_012 (Hydantoin ring opening and decarbamylation) [Hydrothermal]\n  - rxn_006: mol_012, mol_018, mol_019 -> mol_016, mol_020, mol_001 (Native-iron-driven carbon dioxide reduction and C3 assembly) [Hydrothermal]\n  - rxn_007: mol_014, mol_013, mol_001 -> mol_016, mol_015 (Candidate methanethiol-carbon monoxide carbonylation to pyruvate) [Hydrothermal]\n  - rxn_008: mol_016, mol_002, mol_017 -> mol_010, mol_011, mol_001 (Hydrogen-driven reductive amination of pyruvate) [Hydrothermal]\n  - rxn_009: mol_016, mol_002, mol_018, mol_019 -> mol_010, mol_011, mol_020, mol_001 (Native-iron-driven reductive amination of pyruvate) [Hydrothermal]\n  - rxn_010: mol_016, mol_021 -> mol_022, mol_001 (Pyruvate trapping as an oxime) [Hydrothermal]\n  - rxn_011: mol_022, mol_017 -> mol_010, mol_011, mol_001 (Catalytic hydrogenation and deoxygenation of pyruvate oxime) [Hydrothermal]\n  - rxn_012: mol_022, mol_018, mol_019 -> mol_010, mol_011, mol_020, mol_001 (Native-iron reduction of pyruvate oxime) [Hydrothermal]\n\nPATHWAYS (10):\n  - P1: Direct Strecker hydrolysis (3 steps)\n  - P2: Strecker entry through a hydantoin reservoir (4 steps)\n  - P3: CO2-derived pyruvate with ammonia and hydrogen (2 steps)\n  - P4: CO2-derived pyruvate with ammonia and consumed iron (2 steps)\n  - P5: CO2-derived pyruvate through oxime hydrogenation (3 steps)\n  - P6: CO2-derived pyruvate through iron-reduced oxime (3 steps)\n  - P7: Carbonylative pyruvate with ammonia and hydrogen (2 steps)\n  - P8: Carbonylative pyruvate with ammonia and consumed iron (2 steps)\n  - P9: Carbonylative pyruvate through oxime hydrogenation (3 steps)\n  - P10: Carbonylative pyruvate through iron-reduced oxime (3 steps)\n\n\n=== DETERMINISTIC ATOM/CHARGE BALANCE CHECK (RDKit) ===\nComputed programmatically from each molecule's InChI (via RDKit) or stated formula. Reactions list no coefficients, so a reaction counts as balanced if some positive integer coefficients (<= 24) conserve every element and net charge.\nSummary: 12 reactions | 2 balanced as written | 10 balanceable with coefficients | 0 UNBALANCED | 0 unchecked\n  - rxn_002 balanceable: 2 C2H5N + 2 HCN -> C3H6N2 + C3H6N2\n  - rxn_003 balanceable: C3H6N2 + C3H6N2 + 4 H2O -> C3H7NO2 + C3H7NO2 + 2 NH3\n  - rxn_004 ba