"mol_012\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H8NO2+\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"1-carboxyethan-1-aminium\",\n      \"common_name\": \"alaninium ion, racemic population\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Hydrothermal\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H6NO2-\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-aminopropanoate\",\n      \"common_name\": \"alaninate ion, racemic population\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Hydrothermal\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"formula\": \"NH4+\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"azanium\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ammonium\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3/p+1\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Hydrothermal\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_015\",\n      \"formula\": \"CO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"carbon dioxide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"carbon dioxide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CO2/c2-1-3\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_016\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H7NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"(2S)-2-aminopropanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"L-alanine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H7NO2/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h2H,4H2,1H3,(H,5,6)/t2-/m0/s1\",\n      \"role\": \"target\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_017\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H7NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"(2R)-2-aminopropanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"D-alanine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H7NO2/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h2H,4H2,1H3,(H,5,6)/t2-/m1/s1\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Supplementary acetaldehyde supply by pyruvic-acid photodecarboxylation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_005\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_001\",\n        \"mol_015\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-335 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar total pressure\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": false,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic pyruvic-acid vapor above an acidic pool, exposed to near-UV wavelengths overlapping its absorption, approximately 280-350 nm. Capture acetaldehyde in an adjacent cool aqueous reservoir rather than continuing irradiation. No particular conversion or quantum yield is assumed.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Photoexcited pyruvic acid undergoes a decarboxylation channel whose net products are acetaldehyde and carbon dioxide.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Pyruvic-acid photodecarboxylation provides a chemically credible supplementary aldehyde source without a reductant or molecular oxygen. This node represents the productive gas-phase channel, not selective conversion of bulk aqueous pyruvate. Vapor availability, competing photoproducts and capture efficiency make its proposed environmental implementation conditional. The eight pathways beginning with supplied acetaldehyde do not depend on this branch.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Reversible acetaldehyde\u2013ammonia condensation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_001\",\n        \"mol_003\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_008\",\n        \"mol_004\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed pH 8-9.5; total ammonia approximately 0.05-0.5 M and acetaldehyde approximately 1-50 mM in concentrated, shaded porewater. Couple immediately to cyanide capture. Do not assume that ethanimine can be accumulated or isolated from dilute water.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia adds to the aldehyde to form a carbinolamine, followed by reversible dehydration to ethanimine; protonated imine is present according to solution pH.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Imine formation followed by cyanide trapping is the conventional mechanistic description of Strecker chemistry, whose overall alanine synthesis is experimentally established (Strecker, 1850). Water disfavors imine accumulation, so the route relies on trapping a transient intermediate rather than requiring an isolated imine reservoir. No mineral catalyst is needed or asserted.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanide capture of the imine/iminium pool\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_008\",\n        \"mol_002\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_010\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed pH 8-9.5 with approximately 1-50 mM total HCN/CN-. Run concurrently with rxn_002 in a shaded, semiclosed aqueous reservoir that limits HCN volatilization. No chiral agent is present.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanide attacks the planar imine/iminium carbon, followed by proton transfer, furnishing both configurations of 2-aminopropanenitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the carbon\u2013carbon bond-forming step of classical Strecker synthesis (Strecker, 1850). The overlapping availability of ammonia, cyanide and a small iminium population makes mildly alkaline conditions preferable to strongly acidic cyanide addition. Formation is racemic; the L component is retained through subsequent processing.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Acetaldehyde capture as lactonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_001\",\n        \"mol_002\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_009\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed pH 7.5-9 with millimolar-to-tens-of-millimolar acetaldehyde and total cyanide. Limit heating and direct UV exposure. The cyanohydrin remains in reversible equilibrium with acetaldehyde and HCN.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanide adds to the aldehyde carbonyl, and proton transfer produces the racemic cyanohydrin.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Cyanohydrin formation is established carbonyl chemistry and gives an alternative order of reactant capture when cyanide encounters acetaldehyde before substantial ammonia is available. Lactonitrile is not assumed to be a permanent storage compound: dilution, warming and pH changes can release acetaldehyde and HCN.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Ammonia-driven conversion of the lactonitrile pool to alanine nitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_009\",\n        \"mol_003\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_010\",\n        \"mol_004\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-340 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed pH 8-9.5 with a substantial excess of total ammonia, approximately 0.05-0.5 M. Retain dissolved cyanide released by cyanohydrin dissociation. Use shaded, semiclosed porewater rather than assuming efficient conversion in an open dilute pool.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"A lumped cyanohydrin-first Strecker module: reversible lactonitrile dissociation regenerates acetaldehyde and cyanide; ammonia forms an imine/iminium pool that is trapped as the aminonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The branch is an alternative order of substrate capture within established Strecker chemistry, not an unsupported direct substitution of an unactivated hydroxyl group by ammonia. Its microscopic chemistry overlaps rxn_002 and rxn_003. Accordingly, the pathway enumeration distinguishes processing sequences, not independent mechanisms.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Acid-catalyzed hydration of alanine nitrile to alaninamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_010\",\n        \"mol_004\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_011\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Hydronium supplied by anoxic volcanic HCl-bearing water\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"353-393 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"5-20 MPa in a confined shallow hydrothermal pore system\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed acid concentration 0.01-0.3 M H+ equivalents, with chloride as an untracked spectator. Transfer aminonitrile-rich solution only after carbon\u2013nitrogen assembly. Cool before acidification and account for residual free cyanide. Screen residence times from hours to days; alaninamide is an intermediate pool, not necessarily an isolable endpoint.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Nitrile protonation enables water addition, followed by proton transfers and tautomerization to the amide.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Acidic hydrolysis of Strecker aminonitriles is established chemistry (Strecker, 1850). Alaninamide resolves the chemically required hydration stage rather than hiding complete hydrolysis in one equation. Protonation can also suppress reversal of the free aminonitrile. The stated dilute-acid hydrothermal window requires kinetic testing; historical strong-acid work-ups do not establish its yield.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Alkaline hydration of alanine nitrile to alaninamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_010\",\n        \"mol_004\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_011\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Hydroxide supplied by alkaline water\u2013rock interaction fluids\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"383-423 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"5-20 MPa\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydroxide\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed fluid pH 10-11 when measured after cooling to 298 K. In-situ hydroxide activity must be calculated from temperature-dependent water ionization and measured alkalinity. Screen day-to-week residence times in semiclosed pores. Exclude oxidants and monitor aminonitrile loss and competing hydrolysis products.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydroxide attacks the nitrile carbon; proton transfer and imidate-to-amide tautomerization complete hydration and regenerate the catalytic base.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Base-promoted nitrile hydration is chemically established and provides an alternative where alkaline rather than acidic fluids are available. However, vent-like alkalinity is much weaker than many laboratory alkaline hydrolysis mixtures. This is a conditional, potentially slow branch, not a demonstrated quantitative conversion under the stated geochemical window.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Acidic alaninamide hydrolysis to alaninium and ammonium\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_011\",\n        \"mol_004\",\n        \"mol_006\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_012\",\n        \"mol_014\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Hydronium; net proton uptake is also represented explicitly in the inputs\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"383-423 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"5-20 MPa\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed 0.1-1 M acid equivalents in a localized HCl-bearing brine, not the bulk ocean. Screen hours-to-days residence times. The acid budget must exceed residual ammonia and mineral buffering. Rapidly cool the effluent after hydrolysis; avoid black-smoker endmember temperatures.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Acid-catalyzed addition of water to the amide carbonyl and carbon\u2013nitrogen cleavage produce the amino acid and ammonia, which occur predominantly as alaninium and ammonium under sufficiently acidic conditions.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Amide hydrolysis is established, but it can be the slow stage of aminonitrile conversion. The charge-balanced net reaction consumes two proton equivalents per neutral alaninamide molecule, inferred from the listed species. Concentrated anoxic volcanic acid is a localized requirement, not a universal prebiotic assumption. Completion and alanine survival must be measured together.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Alkaline alaninamide hydrolysis to alaninate and ammonia\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_011\",\n        \"mol_007\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_013\",\n        \"mol_003\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"393-433 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"5-20 MPa\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Alkaline porewater, proposed pH 10-11 after cooling to 298 K, with an explicit alkalinity budget supplying at least one hydroxide equivalent per amide hydrolyzed. Determine in-situ speciation separately. Screen day-to-week residence times and quench promptly when conversion is adequate.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydroxide addition to the amide carbonyl gives a tetrahedral intermediate, followed by carbon\u2013nitrogen cleavage and proton transfer to yield alaninate and ammonia.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is ordinary base-promoted amide hydrolysis. Hydroxide is represented as a consumed reactant rather than incorrectly treated as a fully regenerated catalyst. Carboxylate formation favors cleavage, but weak natural alkalinity may make conversion slow. The operating window must be tested for hydrolysis versus deamination, racemization and thermal decomposition; high yield is not assumed.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Acidic effluent equilibration to net-neutral L- and D-alanine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_012\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_016\",\n        \"mol_017\",\n        \"mol_006\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-325 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Cool acidic hydrothermal effluent and mix with adequately buffered water to reach pH 5.5-7.5. Proton removal by the receiving fluid is required to maintain this pH. Store in shaded pores or beneath UV-attenuating sediment.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Carboxyl deprotonation converts alaninium to the net-neutral alanine population, predominantly zwitterionic in water, without changing the stereocenter.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is acid\u2013base equilibration, not a new carbon-skeleton synthesis or chiral resolution. Both enantiomer IDs appear once; inferred coefficients represent conversion of a racemic ion population into equal L and D populations. The standard neutral-form InChIs identify the amino acids, whose dominant aqueous form near neutral pH is zwitterionic.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Alkaline effluent equilibration to net-neutral L- and D-alanine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_013\",\n        \"mol_006\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_016\",\n        \"mol_017\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-325 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Cool alkaline effluent and mix with mildly acidic water to reach pH 5.5-7.5. The supplied acid must first satisfy the residual alkalinity budget. Protect the resulting amino-acid solution from sustained UV exposure and reheating.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Protonation of alaninate gives the net-neutral, predominantly zwitterionic amino-acid population while preserving the existing stereocenters.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Acid\u2013base equilibration is chemically secure once alaninate has formed. It does not enrich L-alanine: the target is the L component of the racemic product. Proton consumption is explicit, and no unsupported mineral-mediated enantioselection is introduced.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Imine-first Strecker entry with fully acidic hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Supplied acetaldehyde forms the transient imine, cyanide traps the aminonitrile, and acidic hydration and amide cleavage produce alaninium. Cooling and pH equilibration yield L-alanine together with D-alanine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_010\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_008\",\n        \"mol_010\",\n        \"mol_011\",\n        \"mol_012\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanohydrin-first Strecker entry with fully acidic hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Acetaldehyde is first captured as lactonitrile. An ammonia-rich receiving solution redirects the reversible cyanohydrin pool into aminonitrile, followed by acidic hydration, acidic amide cleavage and final pH equilibration.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_004\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_010\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_009\",\n        \"mol_010\",\n        \"mol_011\",\n        \"mol_012\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Imine-first Strecker entry with fully alkaline hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Imine capture produces alanine nitrile, which undergoes alkaline hydration and amide cleavage. This branch avoids concentrated acidic hydrolysis but remains conditional on sufficiently rapid conversion at attainable natural alkalinity.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_011\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_008\",\n        \"mol_010\",\n        \"mol_011\",\n        \"mol_013\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanohydrin-first Strecker entry with fully alkaline hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Lactonitrile capture precedes ammonia incorporation. The resulting aminonitrile is processed in alkaline hydrothermal water and cooled before protonation to the net-neutral amino-acid population.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_004\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_011\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_009\",\n        \"mol_010\",\n        \"mol_011\",\n        \"mol_013\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Imine-first entry, acidic nitrile hydration and alkaline amide cleavage\",\n      \"description\": \"Acidic hydration is used to capture the aminonitrile as the less labile amide before transfer to alkaline water for carbon\u2013nitrogen cleavage. The pH switch requires a measured neutralization and alkalinity budget; it is an alternative processing sequence, not a claim of superior yield.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_011\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_008\",\n        \"mol_010\",\n        \"mol_011\",\n        \"mol_013\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanohydrin-first entry, acidic nitrile hydration and alkaline amide cleavage\",\n      \"description\": \"The cyanohydrin-first Strecker sequence supplies aminonitrile. Acidic hydration precedes an amide-stage switch into alkaline hydrothermal water, followed by cooling and final protonation.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_004\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_011\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_009\",\n        \"mol_010\",\n        \"mol_011\",\n        \"mol_013\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Imine-first entry, alkaline nitrile hydration and acidic amide cleavage\",\n      \"description\": \"Alkaline water hydrates the imine-derived aminonitrile. If amide cleavage is too slow there, an acidic mixing zone supplies a different hydrolysis regime after hydration. This option depends on adequate acid surviving the incoming fluid's buffering demand.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_010\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_008\",\n        \"mol_010\",\n        \"mol_011\",\n        \"mol_012\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanohydrin-first entry, alkaline nitrile hydration and acidic amide cleavage\",\n      \"description\": \"Lactonitrile is redirected into alanine nitrile and hydrated under alkaline conditions. Amide-bearing fluid subsequently enters an acidic hydrolysis zone, then cools and equilibrates to the alanine population.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_004\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_010\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_009\",\n        \"mol_010\",\n        \"mol_011\",\n        \"mol_012\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Photodecarboxylative acetaldehyde supply followed by imine-first acidic processing\",\n      \"description\": \"The optional pyruvic-acid vapor photochemistry supplies acetaldehyde for the imine-first Strecker sequence and acidic hydrolysis. Its additional bottlenecks are vapor-phase aldehyde productivity and transfer into a protected aqueous capture reservoir.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_010\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_001\",\n        \"mol_008\",\n        \"mol_010\",\n        \"mol_011\",\n        \"mol_012\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"Photodecarboxylative acetaldehyde supply followed by cyanohydrin-first alkaline processing\",\n      \"description\": \"Photochemically supplied acetaldehyde is captured as lactonitrile before ammonia incorporation. Alkaline hydration and amide cleavage furnish alaninate, followed by cooling and protonation. Both photochemical supply and weakly alkaline hydrolysis remain conditional modules.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_004\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_011\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_001\",\n        \"mol_009\",\n        \"mol_010\",\n        \"mol_011\",\n        \"mol_013\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\n    \"mol_001\",\n    \"mol_002\",\n    \"mol_003\",\n    \"mol_004\",\n    \"mol_006\",\n    \"mol_010\",\n    \"mol_011\",\n    \"mol_016\",\n    \"mol_017\"\n  ],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_001\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_004\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_002\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_004\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_009\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_005\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_005\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_008\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_010\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_011\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_005\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_007\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_007\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_009\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_016\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_010\",\n        \"rxn_011\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_017\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_010\",\n        \"rxn_011\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    }\n  ]\n}", "parsed_response": {"target_molecule": "L-Alanine", "overall_strategy": "Use acetaldehyde, hydrogen cyanide and ammonia in Strecker chemistry, followed by nitrile hydration and amide hydrolysis. Acetaldehyde is an accepted boundary feedstock; pyruvic-acid photodecarboxylation supplies an optional, less certain supplementary source. Ten pathways are enumerated as distinct condition-resolved reaction sequences: imine-first versus cyanohydrin-first entry, acidic versus alkaline hydration, and acidic versus alkaline amide hydrolysis. These are shared-module processing alternatives, not ten independently demonstrated prebiotic mechanisms. All carbon\u2013nitrogen bond-forming branches are achiral and therefore furnish racemic intermediates. Final reactions explicitly produce both L-alanine and D-alanine; no enantiomeric enrichment or isolation is claimed. Aminonitrile and especially amide hydrolysis are the principal kinetic bottlenecks. Stated operating windows are proposed test conditions, not guaranteed yields. Acid\u2013base changes require mixing with the specified acidic or alkaline fluids, and products must be cooled and shielded from continued UV exposure. H+ and OH- represent supplied acid/base equivalents; spectator counterions are omitted. The network deliberately excludes the previously uncertain CO/thiol-to-pyruvate, unspecified hydrogenation, oxime-reduction and hydantoin-release modules.", "molecules": [{"id": "mol_001", "formula": "C2H4O", "iupac_name": "ethanal", "common_name": "acetaldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H4O/c1-2-3/h2H,1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_002", "formula": "CHN", "iupac_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "common_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_003", "formula": "NH3", "iupac_name": "azane", "common_name": "ammonia", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_004", "formula": "H2O", "iupac_name": "oxidane", "common_name": "water", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_005", "formula": "C3H4O3", "iupac_name": "2-oxopropanoic acid", "common_name": "pyruvic acid", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H4O3/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h1H3,(H,5,6)", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_006", "formula": "H+", "iupac_name": "hydrogen(1+)", "common_name": "proton", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_007", "formula": "HO-", "iupac_name": "hydroxide", "common_name": "hydroxide ion", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_008", "formula": "C2H5N", "iupac_name": "ethanimine", "common_name": "acetaldimine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H5N/c1-2-3/h2-3H,1H3", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_009", "formula": "C3H5NO", "iupac_name": "2-hydroxypropanenitrile", "common_name": "lactonitrile", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H5NO/c1-3(5)2-4/h3,5H,1H3", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_010", "formula": "C3H6N2", "iupac_name": "2-aminopropanenitrile", "common_name": "alanine nitrile", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H6N2/c1-3(5)2-4/h3H,5H2,1H3", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_011", "formula": "C3H8N2O", "iupac_name": "2-aminopropanamide", "common_name": "alaninamide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H8N2O/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h2H,4H2,1H3,(H2,5,6)", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_012", "formula": "C3H8NO2+", "iupac_name": "1-carboxyethan-1-aminium", "common_name": "alaninium ion, racemic population", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_013", "formula": "C3H6NO2-", "iupac_name": "2-aminopropanoate", "common_name": "alaninate ion, racemic population", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_014", "formula": "NH4+", "iupac_name": "azanium", "common_name": "ammonium", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3/p+1", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_015", "formula": "CO2", "iupac_name": "carbon dioxide", "common_name": "carbon dioxide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CO2/c2-1-3", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_016", "formula": "C3H7NO2", "iupac_name": "(2S)-2-aminopropanoic acid", "common_name": "L-alanine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H7NO2/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h2H,4H2,1H3,(H,5,6)/t2-/m0/s1", "role": "target", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_017", "formula": "C3H7NO2", "iupac_name": "(2R)-2-aminopropanoic acid", "common_name": "D-alanine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H7NO2/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h2H,4H2,1H3,(H,5,6)/t2-/m1/s1", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}], "reactions": [{"id": "rxn_001", "name": "Supplementary acetaldehyde supply by pyruvic-acid photodecarboxylation", "inputs": ["mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_001", "mol_015"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-335 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar total pressure", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": false, "other": "Anoxic pyruvic-acid vapor above an acidic pool, exposed to near-UV wavelengths overlapping its absorption, approximately 280-350 nm. Capture acetaldehyde in an adjacent cool aqueous reservoir rather than continuing irradiation. No particular conversion or quantum yield is assumed."}, "mechanism": "Photoexcited pyruvic acid undergoes a decarboxylation channel whose net products are acetaldehyde and carbon dioxide.", "reasoning": "Pyruvic-acid photodecarboxylation provides a chemically credible supplementary aldehyde source without a reductant or molecular oxygen. This node represents the productive gas-phase channel, not selective conversion of bulk aqueous pyruvate. Vapor availability, competing photoproducts and capture efficiency make its proposed environmental implementation conditional. The eight pathways beginning with supplied acetaldehyde do not depend on this branch."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Reversible acetaldehyde\u2013ammonia condensation", "inputs": ["mol_001", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_008", "mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 8-9.5; total ammonia approximately 0.05-0.5 M and acetaldehyde approximately 1-50 mM in concentrated, shaded porewater. Couple immediately to cyanide capture. Do not assume that ethanimine can be accumulated or isolated from dilute water."}, "mechanism": "Ammonia adds to the aldehyde to form a carbinolamine, followed by reversible dehydration to ethanimine; protonated imine is present according to solution pH.", "reasoning": "Imine formation followed by cyanide trapping is the conventional mechanistic description of Strecker chemistry, whose overall alanine synthesis is experimentally established (Strecker, 1850). Water disfavors imine accumulation, so the route relies on trapping a transient intermediate rather than requiring an isolated imine reservoir. No mineral catalyst is needed or asserted."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Cyanide capture of the imine/iminium pool", "inputs": ["mol_008", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_010"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 8-9.5 with approximately 1-50 mM total HCN/CN-. Run concurrently with rxn_002 in a shaded, semiclosed aqueous reservoir that limits HCN volatilization. No chiral agent is present."}, "mechanism": "Cyanide attacks the planar imine/iminium carbon, followed by proton transfer, furnishing both configurations of 2-aminopropanenitrile.", "reasoning": "This is the carbon\u2013carbon bond-forming step of classical Strecker synthesis (Strecker, 1850). The overlapping availability of ammonia, cyanide and a small iminium population makes mildly alkaline conditions preferable to strongly acidic cyanide addition. Formation is racemic; the L component is retained through subsequent processing."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Acetaldehyde capture as lactonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_001", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_009"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 7.5-9 with millimolar-to-tens-of-millimolar acetaldehyde and total cyanide. Limit heating and direct UV exposure. The cyanohydrin remains in reversible equilibrium with acetaldehyde and HCN."}, "mechanism": "Cyanide adds to the aldehyde carbonyl, and proton transfer produces the racemic cyanohydrin.", "reasoning": "Cyanohydrin formation is established carbonyl chemistry and gives an alternative order of reactant capture when cyanide encounters acetaldehyde before substantial ammonia is available. Lactonitrile is not assumed to be a permanent storage compound: dilution, warming and pH changes can release acetaldehyde and HCN."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Ammonia-driven conversion of the lactonitrile pool to alanine nitrile", "inputs": ["mol_009", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-340 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 8-9.5 with a substantial excess of total ammonia, approximately 0.05-0.5 M. Retain dissolved cyanide released by cyanohydrin dissociation. Use shaded, semiclosed porewater rather than assuming efficient conversion in an open dilute pool."}, "mechanism": "A lumped cyanohydrin-first Strecker module: reversible lactonitrile dissociation regenerates acetaldehyde and cyanide; ammonia forms an imine/iminium pool that is trapped as the aminonitrile.", "reasoning": "The branch is an alternative order of substrate capture within established Strecker chemistry, not an unsupported direct substitution of an unactivated hydroxyl group by ammonia. Its microscopic chemistry overlaps rxn_002 and rxn_003. Accordingly, the pathway enumeration distinguishes processing sequences, not independent mechanisms."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Acid-catalyzed hydration of alanine nitrile to alaninamide", "inputs": ["mol_010", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_011"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Hydronium supplied by anoxic volcanic HCl-bearing water"], "conditions": {"temperature": "353-393 K", "pressure": "5-20 MPa in a confined shallow hydrothermal pore system", "catalyst": "Hydronium", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed acid concentration 0.01-0.3 M H+ equivalents, with chloride as an untracked spectator. Transfer aminonitrile-rich solution only after carbon\u2013nitrogen assembly. Cool before acidification and account for residual free cyanide. Screen residence times from hours to days; alaninamide is an intermediate pool, not necessarily an isolable endpoint."}, "mechanism": "Nitrile protonation enables water addition, followed by proton transfers and tautomerization to the amide.", "reasoning": "Acidic hydrolysis of Strecker aminonitriles is established chemistry (Strecker, 1850). Alaninamide resolves the chemically required hydration stage rather than hiding complete hydrolysis in one equation. Protonation can also suppress reversal of the free aminonitrile. The stated dilute-acid hydrothermal window requires kinetic testing; historical strong-acid work-ups do not establish its yield."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Alkaline hydration of alanine nitrile to alaninamide", "inputs": ["mol_010", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_011"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Hydroxide supplied by alkaline water\u2013rock interaction fluids"], "conditions": {"temperature": "383-423 K", "pressure": "5-20 MPa", "catalyst": "Hydroxide", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed fluid pH 10-11 when measured after cooling to 298 K. In-situ hydroxide activity must be calculated from temperature-dependent water ionization and measured alkalinity. Screen day-to-week residence times in semiclosed pores. Exclude oxidants and monitor aminonitrile loss and competing hydrolysis products."}, "mechanism": "Hydroxide attacks the nitrile carbon; proton transfer and imidate-to-amide tautomerization complete hydration and regenerate the catalytic base.", "reasoning": "Base-promoted nitrile hydration is chemically established and provides an alternative where alkaline rather than acidic fluids are available. However, vent-like alkalinity is much weaker than many laboratory alkaline hydrolysis mixtures. This is a conditional, potentially slow branch, not a demonstrated quantitative conversion under the stated geochemical window."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Acidic alaninamide hydrolysis to alaninium and ammonium", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_004", "mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_012", "mol_014"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Hydronium; net proton uptake is also represented explicitly in the inputs"], "conditions": {"temperature": "383-423 K", "pressure": "5-20 MPa", "catalyst": "Hydronium", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed 0.1-1 M acid equivalents in a localized HCl-bearing brine, not the bulk ocean. Screen hours-to-days residence times. The acid budget must exceed residual ammonia and mineral buffering. Rapidly cool the effluent after hydrolysis; avoid black-smoker endmember temperatures."}, "mechanism": "Acid-catalyzed addition of water to the amide carbonyl and carbon\u2013nitrogen cleavage produce the amino acid and ammonia, which occur predominantly as alaninium and ammonium under sufficiently acidic conditions.", "reasoning": "Amide hydrolysis is established, but it can be the slow stage of aminonitrile conversion. The charge-balanced net reaction consumes two proton equivalents per neutral alaninamide molecule, inferred from the listed species. Concentrated anoxic volcanic acid is a localized requirement, not a universal prebiotic assumption. Completion and alanine survival must be measured together."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Alkaline alaninamide hydrolysis to alaninate and ammonia", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_007"], "outputs": ["mol_013", "mol_003"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "393-433 K", "pressure": "5-20 MPa", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Alkaline porewater, proposed pH 10-11 after cooling to 298 K, with an explicit alkalinity budget supplying at least one hydroxide equivalent per amide hydrolyzed. Determine in-situ speciation separately. Screen day-to-week residence times and quench promptly when conversion is adequate."}, "mechanism": "Hydroxide addition to the amide carbonyl gives a tetrahedral intermediate, followed by carbon\u2013nitrogen cleavage and proton transfer to yield alaninate and ammonia.", "reasoning": "This is ordinary base-promoted amide hydrolysis. Hydroxide is represented as a consumed reactant rather than incorrectly treated as a fully regenerated catalyst. Carboxylate formation favors cleavage, but weak natural alkalinity may make conversion slow. The operating window must be tested for hydrolysis versus deamination, racemization and thermal decomposition; high yield is not assumed."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Acidic effluent equilibration to net-neutral L- and D-alanine", "inputs": ["mol_012"], "outputs": ["mol_016", "mol_017", "mol_006"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-325 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Cool acidic hydrothermal effluent and mix with adequately buffered water to reach pH 5.5-7.5. Proton removal by the receiving fluid is required to maintain this pH. Store in shaded pores or beneath UV-attenuating sediment."}, "mechanism": "Carboxyl deprotonation converts alaninium to the net-neutral alanine population, predominantly zwitterionic in water, without changing the stereocenter.", "reasoning": "This is acid\u2013base equilibration, not a new carbon-skeleton synthesis or chiral resolution. Both enantiomer IDs appear once; inferred coefficients represent conversion of a racemic ion population into equal L and D populations. The standard neutral-form InChIs identify the amino acids, whose dominant aqueous form near neutral pH is zwitterionic."}, {"id": "rxn_011", "name": "Alkaline effluent equilibration to net-neutral L- and D-alanine", "inputs": ["mol_013", "mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_016", "mol_017"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-325 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Cool alkaline effluent and mix with mildly acidic water to reach pH 5.5-7.5. The supplied acid must first satisfy the residual alkalinity budget. Protect the resulting amino-acid solution from sustained UV exposure and reheating."}, "mechanism": "Protonation of alaninate gives the net-neutral, predominantly zwitterionic amino-acid population while preserving the existing stereocenters.", "reasoning": "Acid\u2013base equilibration is chemically secure once alaninate has formed. It does not enrich L-alanine: the target is the L component of the racemic product. Proton consumption is explicit, and no unsupported mineral-mediated enantioselection is introduced."}], "pathways": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "name": "Imine-first Strecker entry with fully acidic hydrolysis", "description": "Supplied acetaldehyde forms the transient imine, cyanide traps the aminonitrile, and acidic hydration and amide cleavage produce alaninium. Cooling and pH equilibration yield L-alanine together with D-alanine.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_006", "rxn_008", "rxn_010"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_008", "mol_010", "mol_011", "mol_012"]}, {"pathway_id": "P2", "name": "Cyanohydrin-first Strecker entry with fully acidic hydrolysis", "description": "Acetaldehyde is first captured as lactonitrile. An ammonia-rich receiving solution redirects the reversible cyanohydrin pool into aminonitrile, followed by acidic hydration, acidic amide cleavage and final pH equilibration.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_008", "rxn_010"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_009", "mol_010", "mol_011", "mol_012"]}, {"pathway_id": "P3", "name": "Imine-first Strecker entry with fully alkaline hydrolysis", "description": "Imine capture produces alanine nitrile, which undergoes alkaline hydration and amide cleavage. This branch avoids concentrated acidic hydrolysis but remains conditional on sufficiently rapid conversion at attainable natural alkalinity.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_007", "rxn_009", "rxn_011"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_008", "mol_010", "mol_011", "mol_013"]}, {"pathway_id": "P4", "name": "Cyanohydrin-first Strecker entry with fully alkaline hydrolysis", "description": "Lactonitrile capture precedes ammonia incorporation. The resulting aminonitrile is processed in alkaline hydrothermal water and cooled before protonation to the net-neutral amino-acid population.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_007", "rxn_009", "rxn_011"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_009", "mol_010", "mol_011", "mol_013"]}, {"pathway_id": "P5", "name": "Imine-first entry, acidic nitrile hydration and alkaline amide cleavage", "description": "Acidic hydration is used to capture the aminonitrile as the less labile amide before transfer to alkaline water for carbon\u2013nitrogen cleavage. The pH switch requires a measured neutralization and alkalinity budget; it is an alternative processing sequence, not a claim of superior yield.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_006", "rxn_009", "rxn_011"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_008", "mol_010", "mol_011", "mol_013"]}, {"pathway_id": "P6", "name": "Cyanohydrin-first entry, acidic nitrile hydration and alkaline amide cleavage", "description": "The 