m cited studies?\n- Are literature citations present in reaction reasoning?\n- Does the network acknowledge critiques and known challenges identified in the research?\n- Departures from the research are acceptable when explicitly and soundly justified.\n\n## Scoring Guidelines\n- 0.9-1.0: Excellent network, valid and chemically sound\n- 0.8-0.9: Good network with minor issues\n- 0.7-0.8: Acceptable but with notable concerns\n- 0.5-0.7: Significant problems with validity or chemistry\n- 0.0-0.5: Major issues, network is not viable\n\nA network is APPROVED if overall_confidence >= 0.9\n\nOutput your evaluation as JSON matching the NetworkEvaluation schema.\n", "user_message": "Evaluate this synthesis network:\n\nTARGET MOLECULE: L-Alanine\nOVERALL STRATEGY: Use acetaldehyde, hydrogen cyanide and ammonia in Strecker chemistry, followed by nitrile hydration and amide hydrolysis. Acetaldehyde is an accepted boundary feedstock; pyruvic-acid photodecarboxylation supplies an optional, less certain supplementary source. Ten pathways are enumerated as distinct condition-resolved reaction sequences: imine-first versus cyanohydrin-first entry, acidic versus alkaline hydration, and acidic versus alkaline amide hydrolysis. These are shared-module processing alternatives, not ten independently demonstrated prebiotic mechanisms. All carbon\u2013nitrogen bond-forming branches are achiral and therefore furnish racemic intermediates. Final reactions explicitly produce both L-alanine and D-alanine; no enantiomeric enrichment or isolation is claimed. Aminonitrile and especially amide hydrolysis are the principal kinetic bottlenecks. Stated operating windows are proposed test conditions, not guaranteed yields. Acid\u2013base changes require mixing with the specified acidic or alkaline fluids, and products must be cooled and shielded from continued UV exposure. H+ and OH- represent supplied acid/base equivalents; spectator counterions are omitted. The network deliberately excludes the previously uncertain CO/thiol-to-pyruvate, unspecified hydrogenation, oxime-reduction and hydantoin-release modules.\n\nMOLECULES (17 nodes):\n  - mol_001: C2H4O (acetaldehyde) [starting]\n  - mol_002: CHN (hydrogen cyanide) [starting]\n  - mol_003: NH3 (ammonia) [starting]\n  - mol_004: H2O (water) [starting]\n  - mol_005: C3H4O3 (pyruvic acid) [starting]\n  - mol_006: H+ (proton) [starting]\n  - mol_007: HO- (hydroxide ion) [starting]\n  - mol_008: C2H5N (acetaldimine) [intermediate]\n  - mol_009: C3H5NO (lactonitrile) [intermediate]\n  - mol_010: C3H6N2 (alanine nitrile) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_011: C3H8N2O (alaninamide) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_012: C3H8NO2+ (alaninium ion, racemic population) [intermediate]\n  - mol_013: C3H6NO2- (alaninate ion, racemic population) [intermediate]\n  - mol_014: NH4+ (ammonium) [intermediate]\n  - mol_015: CO2 (carbon dioxide) [intermediate]\n  - mol_016: C3H7NO2 (L-alanine) [target]\n  - mol_017: C3H7NO2 (D-alanine) [intermediate]\n\nREACTIONS (11 nodes):\n  - rxn_001: mol_005 -> mol_001, mol_015 (Supplementary acetaldehyde supply by pyruvic-acid photodecarboxylation) [Surface]\n  - rxn_002: mol_001, mol_003 -> mol_008, mol_004 (Reversible acetaldehyde\u2013ammonia condensation) [Surface]\n  - rxn_003: mol_008, mol_002 -> mol_010 (Cyanide capture of the imine/iminium pool) [Surface]\n  - rxn_004: mol_001, mol_002 -> mol_009 (Acetaldehyde capture as lactonitrile) [Surface]\n  - rxn_005: mol_009, mol_003 -> mol_010, mol_004 (Ammonia-driven conversion of the lactonitrile pool to alanine nitrile) [Surface]\n  - rxn_006: mol_010, mol_004 -> mol_011 (Acid-catalyzed hydration of alanine nitrile to alaninamide) [Hydrothermal]\n  - rxn_007: mol_010, mol_004 -> mol_011 (Alkaline hydration of alanine nitrile to alaninamide) [Hydrothermal]\n  - rxn_008: mol_011, mol_004, mol_006 -> mol_012, mol_014 (Acidic alaninamide hydrolysis to alaninium and ammonium) [Hydrothermal]\n  - rxn_009: mol_011, mol_007 -> mol_013, mol_003 (Alkaline alaninamide hydrolysis to alaninate and ammonia) [Hydrothermal]\n  - rxn_010: mol_012 -> mol_016, mol_017, mol_006 (Acidic effluent equilibration to net-neutral L- and D-alanine) [Surface]\n  - rxn_011: mol_013, mol_006 -> mol_016, mol_017 (Alkaline effluent equilibration to net-neutral L- and D-alanine) [Surface]\n\nPATHWAYS (10):\n  - P1: Imine-first Strecker entry with fully acidic hydrolysis (5 steps)\n  - P2: Cyanohydrin-first Strecker entry with fully acidic hydrolysis (5 steps)\n  - P3: Imine-first Strecker entry with fully alkaline hydrolysis (5 steps)\n  - P4: Cyanohydrin-first Strecker entry with fully alkaline hydrolysis (5 steps)\n  - P5: Imine-first entry, acidic nitrile hydration and alkaline amide cleavage (5 steps)\n  - P6: Cyanohydrin-first entry, acidic nitrile hydration and alkaline amide cleavage (5 steps)\n  - P7: Imine-first entry, alkaline nitrile hydration and acidic amide cleavage (5 steps)\n  - P8: Cyanohydrin-first entry, alkaline nitrile hydration and acidic amide cleavage (5 steps)\n  - P9: Photodecarboxylative acetaldehyde supply followed by imine-first acidic processing (6 steps)\n  - P10: Photodecarboxylative acetaldehyde supply followed by cyanohydrin-first alkaline processing (6 steps)\n\n\n=== DETERMINISTIC ATOM/CHARGE BALANCE CHECK (RDKit) ===\nComputed programmatically from each molecule's InChI (via RDKit) or stated formula. Reactions list no coefficients, so a reaction counts as balanced if some positive integer coefficients (<= 24) conserve every element and net charge.\nSummary: 11 reactions | 8 balanced as written | 3 balanceable with coefficients | 0 UNBALANCED | 0 unchecked\n  - rxn_008 balanceable: C3H8N2O + H2O + 2 H+ -> C3H8NO2+ + NH4+\n  - rxn_010 balanceable: 2 C3H8NO2+ -> C3H7NO2 + C3H7NO2 + 2 H+\n  - rxn_011 balanceable: 2 C3H6NO2- + 2 H+ -> C3H7NO2 + C3H7NO2\nTreat this check as ground truth for mass and charge conservation. Reactions that cannot be balanced violate conservation: reflect this in chemical_feasibility and 