hese feedstock concentrations and their retention are assumptions to test, not inferred environmental abundances.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Net: acetaldehyde + HCN + NH3 -> alanine nitrile + H2O. Reversible ammonia addition and dehydration generate an imine/iminium population that is trapped by cyanide. The reaction node lumps the established Strecker sequence rather than representing an elementary collision. The aminonitrile is an unresolved R/S population.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The acetaldehyde Strecker route is the strongest carbon-nitrogen assembly route in this network (Strecker, 1850; Miller, 1957). No mineral is required or assigned without evidence. The specified pH permits both cyanide nucleophilicity and an appreciable unprotonated ammonia fraction. Volatile losses, competing cyanide chemistry and aminonitrile accumulation must be measured; neither pool concentration nor quantitative capture is guaranteed.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Reversible acetaldehyde capture as lactonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-313 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed window: pH 8.0-9.0; acetaldehyde 5-50 mM; total cyanide 10-100 mM; equilibration monitored over 1-48 hours. Same anoxic, shaded, low-headspace porewater setting as rxn_001. No irradiation step is assumed.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Net: acetaldehyde + HCN -> lactonitrile. Cyanide from the HCN/CN- equilibrium adds to the carbonyl; proton transfer gives the cyanohydrin. Formation and dissociation are reversible.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Cyanohydrin equilibria are relevant to classical aqueous Strecker chemistry and its prebiotic interpretation (Miller, 1957). This is a possible reservoir for supplied acetaldehyde, not an asserted photochemical source. Reduced aldehyde volatility does not establish improved overall retention: cyanohydrin dissociation and cyanide loss must be included in the measured pool balance.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Ammonia-driven transfer from the lactonitrile reservoir to aminonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-323 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed window: pH 8.5-9.3; lactonitrile 5-50 mM; total ammonia/ammonium 0.2-1.0 M; monitor for 1-14 days. Maintain a retained cyanide-containing aqueous pool without gas stripping or UV exposure.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Net pool conversion: lactonitrile + NH3 -> alanine nitrile + H2O. Lactonitrile reversibly releases acetaldehyde and cyanide; aldehyde enters imine formation and cyanide trapping. This is not claimed to be a concerted replacement of hydroxide by ammonia.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The route is supported as coupled solution-pool chemistry, not as an independently established direct substitution mechanism (Strecker, 1850; Miller, 1957). Its distinguishing feature is the initial cyanohydrin reservoir. Conversion requires sufficient ammonia activity and favorable turnover of that reservoir; a balanced net equation alone does not establish its rate or advantage over rxn_001.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Acid-catalyzed hydration of alanine nitrile to alaninamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Aqueous proton, specific acid catalysis\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"383-403 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"30-100 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Aqueous proton\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed window: 0.3-1.0 M free strong-acid equivalents, referenced to a cooled sample; aminonitrile 1-20 mM; monitor over 1-24 hours. Requires an HCl-rich, carbonate-poor geothermal fluid whose acid budget exceeds ammonia and rock-buffering demand. Transfer the preformed aminonitrile solution before heating; formation and hydrolysis are not simultaneous. Acid-stage amines are substantially protonated.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Parent-form net: alanine nitrile + H2O -> alaninamide. Protonation of the nitrile facilitates water addition and conversion of the resulting imidic intermediate to the amide. Rapid amino-group protonation is included in the fluid acid budget rather than represented as an additional carbon-bearing node.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Acid hydrolysis is part of the classical Strecker conversion to alanine (Strecker, 1850). That precedent does not establish selective alaninamide accumulation under this proposed window. Continued hydrolysis can prevent amide buildup. Continuous acid processing does not require amide isolation; pathways that switch pH at the amide stage require measured accumulation and preservation during transfer.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Base-catalyzed hydration of alanine nitrile to alaninamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydroxide ion, nucleophilic hydration catalysis\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"393-413 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"30-100 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydroxide ion\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed window: 0.01-0.10 M free OH- after neutralization demand, corresponding approximately to pH 12-13 at 298 K before activity corrections; aminonitrile 1-20 mM; monitor over 6-168 hours. Requires an unusually alkaline concentrated fluid, not ordinary pH 9-11 vent water. Exclude atmospheric CO2 exchange and quantify rock-buffering losses.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Net: alanine nitrile + H2O -> alaninamide. Hydroxide addition to the nitrile, proton transfer and tautomerization produce the amide. Hydroxide is regenerated in the hydration substep, although subsequent amide hydrolysis consumes hydroxide.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The mechanism follows established nitrile-hydrolysis chemistry, but no condition-matched prebiotic demonstration is supplied for this implementation. Its environmental operating window is therefore conditional. Both incomplete hydration and further amide hydrolysis must be measured. Temperature and alkalinity cannot be inferred from the Hydrothermal label, and ordinary serpentinization fluids are not assumed to provide the stated causticity.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Acidic alaninamide hydrolysis to alaninium and ammonium\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_008\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Aqueous proton, carbonyl activation\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"383-403 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"30-100 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Aqueous proton\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed window: 0.5-2.0 M free strong-acid equivalents; alaninamide 1-20 mM; residence time 24-96 hours, extended only if product stability is verified. For alkaline-to-acid staging, neutralize carried OH-, ammonia and mineral alkalinity before establishing this free-acid concentration. Use anoxic, dark, carbonate-poor fluid and measure residual amide, alanine and degradation products.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Balanced racemic-population equation: 2 alaninamide + 2 H2O + 4 H+ -> L-alaninium + D-alaninium + 2 NH4+. Acid-assisted water addition to the amide carbonyl and C-N cleavage release ammonium. The amino acid is represented in its acidic cationic form.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Amide hydrolysis is a chemically established terminal step in nitrile-to-amino-acid conversion. Classical Strecker chemistry supports the overall transformation (Strecker, 1850), but does not supply a yield for this proposed geothermal matrix. The explicit acid inventory includes product protonation. This is a principal kinetic bottleneck; completion must be demonstrated rather than inferred from heating.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Alkaline alaninamide hydrolysis to alaninate and ammonia\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_009\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_014\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"393-423 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"30-100 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed window: 0.10-0.50 M free OH-; alaninamide 1-20 mM; monitor over 24-168 hours. Hydroxide is a consumed reagent. Acid-to-base staging must first neutralize carried strong acid and protonated solutes, then establish the specified excess OH-. Such caustic fluids require independent geochemical justification. Retain the aqueous phase under pressure and cool after conversion.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Balanced racemic-population equation: 2 alaninamide + 2 OH- -> L-alaninate + D-alaninate + 2 NH3. Hydroxide addition and collapse of the tetrahedral intermediate cleave the amide bond. Carboxylate formation makes hydroxide consumption explicit.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Alkaline amide hydrolysis is established chemistry, but the stated concentration and residence-time window is a proposed implementation rather than a demonstrated early-Earth sequence. Strong alkalinity is the main environmental limitation, and high-temperature side reactions require a product mass balance. No mineral catalyst or guaranteed yield is asserted. Mixed-pH variants are feasible only if their neutralization and dilution budgets close.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Buffered neutralization of alaninium to net-neutral alanine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_009\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_016\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Cool the acidic hydrolysate before mixing with a characterized alkaline fluid. First account for all residual free acid, then adjust the final measured pH to 5.8-6.3. Mixing dilution, adsorption and transfer losses must be measured. Keep the resulting solution anoxic and UV-shielded.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Balanced equation: L-alaninium + D-alaninium + 2 OH- -> L-alanine + D-alanine + 2 H2O. The net-neutral amino acids are predominantly zwitterionic near pH 6. The equation describes amino-acid proton transfer, not the additional neutralization of excess process acid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is ordinary acid-base equilibration, requiring no catalyst. It does not create stereoselectivity, remove D-alanine or establish enantiopure isolation. The explicit workup prevents an acidic alaninium solution from being silently identified with a neutral-pH product solution.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Buffered protonation of alaninate to net-neutral alanine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_014\", \"mol_008\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_016\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Cool the alkaline hydrolysate, neutralize residual free OH- and account for ammonia buffering before establishing pH 5.8-6.3 with an acidic fluid. Do not infer the endpoint from initial mixing ratios alone. Maintain anoxic, UV-shielded conditions.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Balanced equation: L-alaninate + D-alaninate + 2 H+ -> L-alanine + D-alanine. Protonation gives the predominantly zwitterionic, net-neutral amino-acid population near pH 6.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is established acid-base chemistry rather than an additional amino-acid synthesis mechanism. Both enantiomers remain present. The stated bulk-buffer requirement addresses the difference between neutralizing alaninate itself and neutralizing the complete caustic reaction mixture.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Conditional native-iron reductive amination of pyruvate with ammonium\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_017\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_018\", \"mol_008\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_016\", \"mol_019\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"343-363 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar; no externally supplied H2\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screening window: pH 6.0-7.0 maintained at reaction temperature; pyruvate 1-10 mM; NH4Cl 0.1-1.0 M; freshly exposed Fe(0) at 5-20 molar equivalents relative to pyruvate; residence time 24-168 hours. Use anoxic, shaded, low-sulfide, low-carbonate porewater. Quantify Fe corrosion, hydrogen evolution, lactate formation, ammonia availability and iron-surface passivation.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Balanced productive-branch equation: 2 pyruvic acid + 2 NH4+ + 2 Fe + 2 H+ -> L-alanine + D-alanine + 2 Fe2+ + 2 H2O. The unprotonated ammonia fraction forms an imine/iminium population; electron transfer from Fe(0) could reduce its C=N bond. Each alanine requires one Fe atom oxidized from 0 to +2. Fe(0) is a consumed reductant, not a catalyst.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is a speculative, condition-sensitive implementation retained because ammonium is a simpler nitrogen feedstock than hydroxylamine. The specified window does not establish selective or useful alanine production. Evidence for Fe/hydroxylamine chemistry, including Muchowska et al. (2019), must not be treated as validation of direct ammonium amination. The key unresolved competition is imine reduction versus pyruvate reduction and unproductive corrosion. Local native or meteoritic iron must be available; abundant dissolved Fe2+ cannot substitute for Fe(0).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Capture of pyruvic acid as its oxime\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_017\", \"mol_020\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_021\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-323 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed window: pH 4.5-6.5; pyruvate 1-20 mM; hydroxylamine 1.2-3 molar equivalents; residence time 1-24 hours. Use anoxic, shaded water with no assumed photochemical replenishment. Quantify oxime formation before reduction and include any E/Z mixture in the analytical pool.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Net: pyruvic acid + NH2OH -> pyruvic acid oxime + H2O. Hydroxylamine adds to the keto carbonyl, followed by proton transfer and dehydration. No mineral or additional catalyst is required for this proposed condensation.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Oxime formation is established carbonyl chemistry and provides a more persistent nitrogen-bearing reduction substrate than an ammonia-derived imine. Hydroxylamine-associated conversion of keto acids to amino acids has experimental precedent in iron-promoted protometabolic work (Muchowska et al., 2019). Hydroxylamine is an explicitly supplied feedstock here; its origin, persistence under early-Earth UV and environmental concentration are not established by this network.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Native-iron reduction of pyruvate oxime to alanine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_021\", \"mol_018\", \"mol_008\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_016\", \"mol_019\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"343-363 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar; no externally supplied H2\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screening window: pH 4.0-6.0 maintained at reaction temperature; oxime 0.5-10 mM; freshly exposed Fe(0) at 5-20 molar equivalents; residence time 24-168 hours. Keep the mixture anoxic, shaded, low in sulfide and carbonate, and free of assumed CO/thiol carryover. Measure residual oxime, partially reduced nitrogen species, alanine, Fe2+ and corrosion losses.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Balanced racemic-population equation: 2 pyruvic acid oxime + 4 Fe + 8 H+ -> L-alanine + D-alanine + 4 Fe2+ + 2 H2O. Complete conversion requires both C=N reduction and N-O bond cleavage, totaling four electrons and two Fe atoms per oxime. The listed product pair represents a racemic population, not production of two alanines from one oxime.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Iron/hydroxylamine-based amino-acid generation from keto-acid systems has laboratory precedent (Muchowska et al., 2019), supporting this reaction class more directly than rxn_010. That precedent does not provide a yield for the specified natural-water matrix or prove quantitative reduction of an independently accumulated oxime pool. Completion of both reduction events, proton supply and surface activity must be demonstrated. Ferrous ion is serialized as Fe+2 and InChI=1S/Fe/q+2, unambiguously one Fe atom with charge +2.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct Strecker capture with continuous acidic hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Acetaldehyde, cyanide and ammonia form alanine nitrile, followed by acidic hydration and amide hydrolysis. Cooling and buffered neutralization yield L-alanine together with D-alanine. No isolated alaninamide pool is required. This is the strongest classical route family, although the stated environmental operating window still requires validation.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_011\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct Strecker capture with continuous alkaline hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"The same aminonitrile enters alkaline hydration and amide cleavage, followed by cooling and protonation of alaninate. The chemistry does not require amide isolation, but sufficiently caustic fluid and an adequate hydrolysis residence time are conditional environmental requirements.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct Strecker capture with acidic hydration and alkaline amide cleavage\",\n      \"description\": \"Acidic nitrile hydration is followed by alkaline amide hydrolysis. This is a conditional pH-history variant, not an independent mechanism or a claimed yield improvement. It requires measured alaninamide accumulation, survival during mixing, and enough base to neutralize carried acid before establishing the alkaline reaction window.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct Strecker capture with alkaline hydration and acidic amide cleavage\",\n      \"description\": \"Alkaline nitrile hydration is followed by acidic amide hydrolysis and product neutralization. This conditional staging variant is valid only if amide survives the transition and acid supply exceeds carried hydroxide, ammonia and mineral buffering. A pH gradient alone does not establish those requirements.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_011\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Lactonitrile reservoir with continuous acidic hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Acetaldehyde is first captured as lactonitrile. Ammonia-driven pool turnover supplies alanine nitrile, which undergoes continuous acidic hydrolysis and buffered neutralization. The reservoir is reversible; improved volatile retention is a testable possibility, not an assumed performance benefit.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_011\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Lactonitrile reservoir with continuous alkaline hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"The cyanohydrin pool feeds aminonitrile formation, followed by alkaline hydration, alkaline amide cleavage and protonation. Feasibility depends on reservoir turnover, cyanide retention and availability of a sufficiently alkaline processing fluid.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Lactonitrile reservoir with acidic hydration and alkaline amide cleavage\",\n      \"description\": \"Reservoir-mediated aminonitrile formation is combined with acid-to-base hydrolysis staging. This route requires both productive cyanohydrin turnover and a measurable amide pool that survives neutralization and dilution. It is an operating-history alternative rather than a separately demonstrated prebiotic synthesis.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Lactonitrile reservoir with alkaline hydration and acidic amide cleavage\",\n      \"description\": \"Reservoir-mediated aminonitrile formation is followed by base-to-acid hydrolysis staging and final neutralization. Amide survival, acid-buffer capacity and transport losses must be demonstrated. It does not establish a new amino-acid-forming mechanism beyond the shared Strecker sequence.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_011\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Supplied pyruvate with conditional ammonium/native-iron reductive amination\",\n      \"description\": \"Supplied pyruvate is combined with ammonium and consumed Fe(0). The productive net reaction is balanced, but selective direct amination under the specified conditions remains speculative. This pathway does not assume pyruvate synthesis from CO2 or an unidentified H2-activation catalyst.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_010\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"Supplied pyruvate through an oxime and native-iron reduction\",\n      \"description\": \"Hydroxylamine traps supplied pyruvate as an oxime, followed by four-electron reduction using two stoichiometric Fe atoms per alanine at the theoretical minimum. The reaction class has laboratory precedent, but complete reduction, reductant efficiency and feedstock persistence require matrix-specific verification.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_021\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\n    \"mol_003\",\n    \"mol_004\",\n    \"mol_006\",\n    \"mol_007\",\n    \"mol_008\",\n    \"mol_009\",\n    \"mol_010\",\n    \"mol_015\",\n    \"mol_016\",\n    \"mol_017\",\n    \"mol_018\",\n    \"mol_019\"\n  ],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_003\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_007\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_015\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_016\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_019\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_010\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    }\n  ]\n}", "parsed_response": {"target_molecule": "L-Alanine", "overall_strategy": "Use supplied acetaldehyde, cyanide and ammonia for Strecker chemistry, with direct capture and a reversible lactonitrile reservoir as alternative entry routes. Resolve aminonitrile hydrolysis into hydration and amide hydrolysis, with explicitly different acidic and alkaline operating histories. Eight pathways enumerate these combinations; they are not eight independent prebiotic mechanisms. Two additional pathways start from supplied pyruvic acid and use consumed native iron for direct reductive amination or oxime reduction. No selective CO2-to-pyruvate synthesis, unspecified H2-activation catalyst, hydantoin-release step or photochemical acetaldehyde source is assumed. All productive routes are achiral and yield L-alanine together with D-alanine; enantiopure isolation is not claimed. The numerical conditions below are proposed operating windows, not reported yields or demonstrations of complete environmental sequences. Mixed-pH staging, concentrated alkaline hydrolysis and direct ammonium/iron amination remain conditional. Surface chemistry requires UV-shielded porewater and retention of volatile reagents. Hydrothermal processing requires separate, chemically characterized fluids rather than an unspecified change of environment. Intermediate formulas use analytical parent forms where rapid protonation equilibria are implicit; terminal alanine charge states are represented explicitly. Coefficients are written in mechanism descriptions rather than repeated molecule IDs.", "molecules": [{"id": "mol_001", "formula": "C2H4O", "iupac_name": "Ethanal", "common_name": "Acetaldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H4O/c1-2-3/h2H,1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_002", "formula": "CHN", "iupac_name": "Hydrogen cyanide", "common_name": "Hydrogen cyanide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_003", "formula": "H3N", "iupac_name": "Azane", "common_name": "Ammonia", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_004", "formula": "H2O", "iupac_name": "Oxidane", "common_name": "Water", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_005", "formula": "C3H5NO", "iupac_name": "2-Hydroxypropanenitrile", "common_name": "Lactonitrile", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_006", "formula": "C3H6N2", "iupac_name": "2-Aminopropanenitrile", "common_name": "Alanine nitrile", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_007", "formula": "C3H8N2O", "iupac_name": "2-Aminopropanamide", "common_name": "Alaninamide", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_008", "formula": "H+", "iupac_name": "Hydron", "common_name": "Aqueous proton equivalent", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_009", "formula": "HO-", "iupac_name": "Hydroxide", "common_name": "Hydroxide ion", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_010", "formula": "H4N+", "iupac_name": "Azanium", "common_name": "Ammonium", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_011", "formula": "C3H8NO2+", "iupac_name": "(1S)-1-Carboxyethan-1-aminium", "common_name": "L-Alaninium", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_012", "formula": "C3H8NO2+", "iupac_name": "(1R)-1-Carboxyethan-1-aminium", "common_name": "D-Alaninium", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_013", "formula": "C3H6NO2-", "iupac_name": "(2S)-2-Aminopropanoate", "common_name": "L-Alaninate", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_014", "formula": "C3H6NO2-", "iupac_name": "(2R)-2-Aminopropanoate", "common_name": "D-Alaninate", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_015", "formula": "C3H7NO2", "iupac_name": "(2S)-2-Aminopropanoic acid", "common_name": "L-Alanine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H7NO2/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h2H,4H2,1H3,(H,5,6)/t2-/m0/s1", "role": "target", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_016", "formula": "C3H7NO2", "iupac_name": "(2R)-2-Aminopropanoic acid", "common_name": "D-Alanine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H7NO2/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h2H,4H2,1H3,(H,5,6)/t2-/m1/s1", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_017", "formula": "C3H4O3", "iupac_name": "2-Oxopropanoic acid", "common_name": "Pyruvic acid", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H4O3/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h1H3,(H,5,6)", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_018", "formula": "Fe", "iupac_name": "Iron", "common_name": "Native iron, Fe(0)", "inchi": "InChI=1S/Fe", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_019", "formula": "Fe+2", "iupac_name": "Iron(2+)", "common_name": "Ferrous ion", "inchi": "InChI=1S/Fe/q+2", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_020", "formula": "H3NO", "iupac_name": "Hydroxylamine", "common_name": "Hydroxylamine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3NO/c1-2/h2H,1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_021", "formula": "C3H5NO3", "iupac_name": "2-(Hydroxyimino)propanoic acid", "common_name": "Pyruvic acid oxime", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}], "reactions": [{"id": "rxn_001", "name": "Direct aqueous Strecker capture of acetaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_001", "mol_002", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_006", "mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-313 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed window: pH 8.5-9.2; acetaldehyde 5-50 mM; total HCN/CN- 10-100 mM; total NH3/NH4+ 0.1-0.5 M; residence time 1-7 days. Use anoxic, UV-shielded, water-saturated pores with little gas-filled volume and no gas flushing. These feedstock concentrations and their retention are assumptions to test, not inferred environmental abundances."}, "mechanism": "Net: acetaldehyde + HCN + NH3 -> alanine nitrile + H2O. Reversible ammonia addition and dehydration generate an imine/iminium population that is trapped by cyanide. The reaction node lumps the established Strecker sequence rather than representing an elementary collision. The aminonitrile is an unresolved R/S population.", "reasoning": "The acetaldehyde Strecker route is the strongest carbon-nitrogen assembly route in this network (Strecker, 1850; Miller, 1957). No mineral is required or assigned without evidence. The specified pH permits both cyanide nucleophilicity and an appreciable unprotonated ammonia fraction. Volatile losses, competing cyanide chemistry and aminonitrile accumulation must be measured; neither pool concentration nor quantitative capture is guaranteed."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Reversible acetaldehyde capture as lactonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_001", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_005"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-313 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed window: pH 8.0-9.0; acetaldehyde 5-50 mM; total cyanide 10-100 mM; equilibration monitored over 1-48 hours. Same anoxic, shaded, low-headspace porewater setting as rxn_001. No irradiation step is assumed."}, "mechanism": "Net: acetaldehyde + HCN -> lactonitrile. Cyanide from the HCN/CN- equilibrium adds to the carbonyl; proton transfer gives the cyanohydrin. Formation and dissociation are reversible.", "reasoning": "Cyanohydrin equilibria are relevant to classical aqueous Strecker chemistry and its prebiotic interpretation (Miller, 1957). This is a possible reservoir for supplied acetaldehyde, not an asserted photochemical source. Reduced aldehyde volatility does not establish improved overall retention: cyanohydrin dissociation and cyanide loss must be included in the measured pool balance."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Ammonia-driven transfer from the lactonitrile reservoir to aminonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_005", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_006", "mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-323 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed window: pH 8.5-9.3; lactonitrile 5-50 mM; total ammonia/ammonium 0.2-1.0 M; monitor for 1-14 days. Maintain a retained cyanide-containing aqueous pool without gas stripping or UV exposure."}, "mechanism": "Net pool conversion: lactonitrile + NH3 -> alanine nitrile + H2O. Lactonitrile reversibly releases acetaldehyde and cyanide; aldehyde enters imine formation and cyanide trapping. This is not claimed to be a concerted replacement of hydroxide by ammonia.", "reasoning": "The route is supported as coupled solution-pool chemistry, not as an independently established direct substitution mechanism (Strecker, 1850; Miller, 1957). Its distinguishing feature is the initial cyanohydrin reservoir. Conversion requires sufficient ammonia activity and favorable turnover of that reservoir; a balanced net equation alone does not establish its rate or advantage over rxn_001."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Acid-catalyzed hydration of alanine nitrile to alaninamide", "inputs": ["mol_006", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_007"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Aqueous proton, specific acid catalysis"], "conditions": {"temperature": "383-403 K", "pressure": "30-100 bar", "catalyst": "Aqueous proton", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed window: 0.3-1.0 M free strong-acid equivalents, referenced to a cooled sample; aminonitrile 1-20 mM; monitor over 1-24 hours. Requires an HCl-rich, carbonate-poor geothermal fluid whose acid budget exceeds ammonia and rock-buffering demand. Transfer the preformed aminonitrile solution before heating; formation and hydrolysis are not simultaneous. Acid-stage amines are substantially protonated."}, "mechanism": "Parent-form net: alanine nitrile + H2O -> alaninamide. Protonation of the nitrile facilitates water addition and conversion of the resulting imidic intermediate to the amide. Rapid amino-group protonation is included in the fluid acid budget rather than represented as an additional carbon-bearing node.", "reasoning": "Acid hydrolysis is part of the classical Strecker conversion to alanine (Strecker, 1850). That precedent does not establish selective alaninamide accumulation under this proposed window. Continued hydrolysis can prevent amide buildup. Continuous acid processing does not require amide isolation; pathways that switch pH at the amide stage require measured accumulation and preservation during transfer."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Base-catalyzed hydration of alanine nitrile to alaninamide", "inputs": ["mol_006", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_007"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Hydroxide ion, nucleophilic hydration catalysis"], "conditions": {"temperature": "393-413 K", "pressure": "30-100 bar", "catalyst": "Hydroxide ion", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed window: 0.01-0.10 M free OH- after neutralization demand, corresponding approximately to pH 12-13 at 298 K before activity corrections; aminonitrile 1-20 mM; monitor over 6-168 hours. Requires an unusually alkaline concentrated fluid, not ordinary pH 9-11 vent water. Exclude atmospheric CO2 exchange and quantify rock-buffering losses."}, "mechanism": "Net: alanine nitrile + H2O -> alaninamide. Hydroxide addition to the nitrile, proton transfer and tautomerization produce the amide. Hydroxide is regenerated in the hydration substep, although subsequent amide hydrolysis consumes hydroxide.", "reasoning": "The mechanism follows established nitrile-hydrolysis chemistry, but no condition-matched prebiotic demonstration is supplied for this implementation. Its environmental operating window is therefore conditional. Both incomplete hydration and further amide hydrolysis must be measured. Temperature and alkalinity cannot be inferred from the Hydrothermal label, and ordinary serpentinization fluids are not assumed to provide the stated causticity."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Acidic alaninamide hydrolysis to alaninium and ammonium", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_004", "mol_008"], "outputs": ["mol_011", "mol_012", "mol_010"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Aqueous proton, carbonyl activation"], "conditions": {"temperature": "383-403 K", "pressure": "30-100 bar", "catalyst": "Aqueous proton", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed window: 0.5-2.0 M free strong-acid equivalents; alaninamide 1-20 mM; residence time 24-96 hours, extended only if product stability is verified. For alkaline-to-acid staging, neutralize carried OH-, ammonia and mineral alkalinity before establishing this free-acid concentration. Use anoxic, dark, carbonate-poor fluid and measure residual amide, alanine and degradation products."}, "mechanism": "Balanced racemic-population equation: 2 alaninamide + 2 H2O + 4 H+ -> L-alaninium + D-alaninium + 2 NH4+. Acid-assisted water addition to the amide carbonyl and C-N cleavage release ammonium. The amino acid is represented in its acidic cationic form.", "reasoning": "Amide hydrolysis is a chemically established terminal step in nitrile-to-amino-acid conversion. Classical Strecker chemistry supports the overall transformation (Strecker, 1850), but does not supply a yield for this proposed geothermal matrix. The explicit acid inventory includes product protonation. This is a principal kinetic bottleneck; completion must be demonstrated rather than inferred from heating."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Alkaline alaninamide hydrolysis to alaninate and ammonia", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_009"], "outputs": ["mol_013", "mol_014", "mol_003"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "393-423 K", "pressure": "30-100 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed window: 0.10-0.50 M free OH-; alaninamide 1-20 mM; monitor over 24-168 hours. Hydroxide is a consumed reagent. Acid-to-base staging must first neutralize carried strong acid and protonated solutes, then establish the specified excess OH-. Such caustic fluids require independent geochemical justification. Retain the aqueous phase under pressure and cool after conversion."}, "mechanism": "Balanced racemic-population equation: 2 alaninamide + 2 OH- -> L-alaninate + D-alaninate + 2 NH3. Hydroxide addition and collapse of the tetrahedral intermediate cleave the amide bond. Carboxylate formation makes hydroxide consumption explicit.", "reasoning": "Alkaline amide hydrolysis is established chemistry, but the stated concentration and residence-time window is a proposed implementation rather than a demonstrated early-Earth sequence. Strong alkalinity is the main environmental limitation, and high-temperature side reactions require a product mass balance. No mineral catalyst or guaranteed yield is asserted. Mixed-pH variants are feasible only if their neutralization and dilution budgets close."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Buffered neutralization of alaninium to net-neutral alanine", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_012", "mol_009"], "outputs": ["mol_015", "mol_016", "mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Cool the acidic hydrolysate before mixing with a characterized alkaline fluid. First account for all residual free acid, then adjust the final measured pH to 5.8-6.3. Mixing dilution, adsorption and transfer losses must be measured. Keep the resulting solution anoxic and UV-shielded."}, "mechanism": "Balanced equation: L-alaninium + D-alaninium + 2 OH- -> L-alanine + D-alanine + 2 H2O. The net-neutral amino acids are predominantly zwitterionic near pH 6. The equation describes amino-acid proton transfer, not the additional neutralization of excess process acid.", "reasoning": "This is ordinary acid-base equilibration, requiring no catalyst. It does not create stereoselectivity, remove D-alanine or establish enantiopure isolation. The explicit workup prevents an acidic alaninium solution from being silently identified with a neutral-pH product solution."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Buffered protonation of alaninate to net-neutral alanine", "inputs": ["mol_013", "mol_014", "mol_008"], "outputs": ["mol_015", "mol_016"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Cool the alkaline hydrolysate, neutralize residual free OH- and account for ammonia buffering before establishing pH 5.8-6.3 with an acidic fluid. Do not infer the endpoint from initial mixing ratios alone. Maintain anoxic, UV-shielded conditions."}, "mechanism": "Balanced equation: L-alaninate + D-alaninate + 2 H+ -> L-alanine + D-alanine. Protonation gives the predominantly zwitterionic, net-neutral amino-acid population near pH 6.", "reasoning": "This is established acid-base chemistry rather than an additional amino-acid synthesis mechanism. Both enantiomers remain present. The stated bulk-buffer requirement addresses the difference between neutralizing alaninate itself and neutralizing the complete caustic reaction mixture."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Conditional native-iron reductive amination of pyruvate with ammonium", "inputs": ["mol_017", "mol_010", "mol_018", "mol_008"], "outputs": ["mol_015", "mol_016", "mol_019", "mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "343-363 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar; no externally supplied H2", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screening window: pH 6.0-7.0 maintained at reaction temperature; pyruvate 1-10 mM; NH4Cl 0.1-1.0 M; freshly exposed Fe(0) at 5-20 molar equivalents relative to pyruvate; residence time 24-168 hours. Use anoxic, shaded, low-sulfide, low-carbonate porewater. Quantify Fe corrosion, hydrogen evolution, lactate formation, ammonia availability and iron-surface passivation."}, "mechanism": "Balanced productive-branch equation: 2 pyruvic acid + 2 NH4+ + 2 Fe + 2 H+ -> L-alanine + D-alanine + 2 Fe2+ + 2 H2O. The unprotonated ammonia fraction forms an imine/iminium population; electron transfer from Fe(0) could reduce its C=N bond. Each alanine requires one Fe atom oxidized from 0 to +2. Fe(0) is a consumed reductant, not a catalyst.", "reasoning": "This is a speculative, condition-sensitive implementation retained because ammonium is a simpler nitrogen feedstock than hydroxylamine. The specified window does not establish selective or useful alanine production. Evidence for Fe/hydroxylamine chemistry, including Muchowska et al. (2019), must not be treated as validation of direct ammonium amination. The key unresolved competition is imine reduction versus pyruvate reduction and unproductive corrosion. Local native or meteoritic iron must be available; abundant dissolved Fe2+ cannot substitute for Fe(0)."}, {"id": "rxn_011", "name": "Capture of pyruvic acid as its oxime", "inputs": ["mol_017", "mol_020"], "outputs": ["mol_021", "mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-323 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed window: pH 4.5-6.5; pyruvate 1-20 mM; hydroxylamine 1.2-3 molar equivalents; residence time 1-24 hours. Use anoxic, shaded water with no assumed photochemical replenishment. Quantify oxime formation before reduction and include any E/Z mixture in the analytical pool."}, "mechanism": "Net: pyruvic acid + NH2OH -> pyruvic acid oxime + H2O. Hydroxylamine adds to the keto carbonyl, followed by proton transfer and dehydration. No mineral or additional catalyst is required for this proposed condensation.", "reasoning": "Oxime formation is established carbonyl chemistry and provides a more persistent nitrogen-bearing reduction substrate than an ammonia-derived imine. Hydroxylamine-associated conversion of keto acids to amino acids has experimental precedent in iron-promoted protometabolic work (Muchowska et al., 2019). Hydroxylamine is an explicitly supplied feedstock here; its origin, persistence under early-Earth UV and environmental concentration are not established by this network."}, {"id": "rxn_012", "name": "Native-iron reduction of pyruvate oxime to alanine", "inputs": ["mol_021", "mol_018", "mol_008"], "outputs": ["mol_015", "mol_016", "mol_019", "mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "343-363 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar; no externally supplied H2", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screening window: pH 4.0-6.0 maintained at reaction temperature; oxime 0.5-10 mM; freshly exposed Fe(0) at 5-20 molar equivalents; residence time 24-168 hours. Keep the mixture anoxic, shaded, low in sulfide and carbonate, and free of assumed CO/thiol carryover. Measure residual oxime, partially reduced nitrogen species, alanine, Fe2+ and corrosion losses."}, "mechanism": "Balanced racemic-population equation: 2 pyruvic acid oxime + 4 Fe + 8 H+ -> L-alanine + D-alanine + 4 Fe2+ + 2 H2O. Complete conversion requires both C=N reduction and N-O bond cleavage, totaling four electrons and two Fe atoms per oxime. The listed product pair represents a racemic population, not production of two alanines from one oxime.", "reasoning": "Iron/hydroxylamine-based amino-acid generation from keto-acid systems has laboratory precedent (Muchowska et al., 2019), supporting this reaction class more directly than rxn_010. That precedent does not provide a yield for the specified natural-water matrix or prove quantitative reduction of an independently accumulated oxime pool. Completion of both reduction events, proton supply and surface activity must be demonstrated. Ferrous ion is serialized as Fe+2 and InChI=1S/Fe/q+2, unambiguously one Fe atom with charge +2."}], "pathways": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "name": "Direct Strecker capture with continuous acidic hydrolysis", "description": "Acetaldehyde, cyanide and ammonia form alanine nitrile, followed by acidic hydration and amide hydrolysis. Cooling and buffered neutralization yield L-alanine together with D-alanine. No isolated alaninamide pool is required. This is the strongest classical route family, although