m cited studies?\n- Are literature citations present in reaction reasoning?\n- Does the network acknowledge critiques and known challenges identified in the research?\n- Departures from the research are acceptable when explicitly and soundly justified.\n\n## Scoring Guidelines\n- 0.9-1.0: Excellent network, valid and chemically sound\n- 0.8-0.9: Good network with minor issues\n- 0.7-0.8: Acceptable but with notable concerns\n- 0.5-0.7: Significant problems with validity or chemistry\n- 0.0-0.5: Major issues, network is not viable\n\nA network is APPROVED if overall_confidence >= 0.9\n\nOutput your evaluation as JSON matching the NetworkEvaluation schema.\n", "user_message": "Evaluate this synthesis network:\n\nTARGET MOLECULE: L-Alanine\nOVERALL STRATEGY: Use supplied acetaldehyde, cyanide and ammonia for Strecker chemistry, with direct capture and a reversible lactonitrile reservoir as alternative entry routes. Resolve aminonitrile hydrolysis into hydration and amide hydrolysis, with explicitly different acidic and alkaline operating histories. Eight pathways enumerate these combinations; they are not eight independent prebiotic mechanisms. Two additional pathways start from supplied pyruvic acid and use consumed native iron for direct reductive amination or oxime reduction. No selective CO2-to-pyruvate synthesis, unspecified H2-activation catalyst, hydantoin-release step or photochemical acetaldehyde source is assumed. All productive routes are achiral and yield L-alanine together with D-alanine; enantiopure isolation is not claimed. The numerical conditions below are proposed operating windows, not reported yields or demonstrations of complete environmental sequences. Mixed-pH staging, concentrated alkaline hydrolysis and direct ammonium/iron amination remain conditional. Surface chemistry requires UV-shielded porewater and retention of volatile reagents. Hydrothermal processing requires separate, chemically characterized fluids rather than an unspecified change 