ients (<= 24) conserve every element and net charge.\nSummary: 12 reactions | 6 balanced as written | 6 balanceable with coefficients | 0 UNBALANCED | 0 unchecked\n  - rxn_006 balanceable: 2 C3H8N2O + 2 H2O + 4 H+ -> C3H8NO2+ + C3H8NO2+ + 2 H4N+\n  - rxn_007 balanceable: 2 C3H8N2O + 2 HO- -> C3H6NO2- + C3H6NO2- + 2 H3N\n  - rxn_008 balanceable: C3H8NO2+ + C3H8NO2+ + 2 HO- -> C3H7NO2 + C3H7NO2 + 2 H2O\n  - rxn_009 balanceable: C3H6NO2- + C3H6NO2- + 2 H+ -> C3H7NO2 + C3H7NO2\n  - rxn_010 balanceable: 2 C3H4O3 + 2 H4N+ + 2 Fe + 2 H+ -> C3H7NO2 + C3H7NO2 + 2 Fe+2 + 2 H2O\n  - rxn_012 balanceable: 2 C3H5NO3 + 4 Fe + 8 H+ -> C3H7NO2 + C3H7NO2 + 4 Fe+2 + 2 H2O\nTreat this check as ground truth for mass and charge conservation. Reactions that cannot be balanced violate conservation: reflect this in chemical_feasibility and reaction_scores, list them in problematic_reactions, and name the specific reaction IDs (with the missing species, if hinted) in your feedback so the network can be corrected. Note that implausibly large inferred coefficients may also indicate a mis-specified reaction.\n=== END BALANCE CHECK ===\n\n\nPlease evaluate the network and respond with JSON:\n{\n    \"pathway_evaluations\": [\n        {\n            \"pathway_id\": \"<id>\",\n            \"reaction_scores\": {\"rxn_001\": 0.85, ...},\n            \"chemical_feasibility\": <0.0-1.0>,\n            \"pathway_coherence\": <0.0-1.0>,\n            \"environmental_progression\": <0.0-1.0>,\n            \"target_achievement\": <0.0-1.0>,\n            \"overall_confidence\": <0.0-1.0>,\n            \"feedback\": \"<pathway-specific feedback>\",\n            \"approved\": <true if confidence >= 0.8>,\n            \"problematic_reactions\": [\"rxn_ids with issues\"],\n            \"suggestions\": [\"improvements\"]\n        }\n    ],\n    \"best_pathway_id\": \"<id of best pathway>\",\n    \"overall_confidence\": <0.0-1.0>,\n    \"network_feedback\": \"<overall assessment>\"\n}\n\nNote: \"environmental_progression\" scores Environmental Consistency (conditions fit each\nreaction's stated environment), not any particular order of environments.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"pathway_evaluations\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_001\": 0.91,\n        \"rxn_004\": 0.9,\n        \"rxn_006\": 0.94,\n        \"rxn_008\": 0.98\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.92,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.94,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.7,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.88,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.86,\n      \"feedback\": \"Direct Strecker capture followed by acidic nitrile and amide hydrolysis is chemically coherent. Alaninamide can be a transient intermediate; selective isolation is unnecessary. The route produces L-alanine within a racemic mixture. The principal uncertainty is the unspecified operating conditions and material transfer, not conservation or the underlying transformations.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Specify capture pH, temperature, reagent concentrations and a physically supported means of retaining acetaldehyde, HCN and ammonia.\",\n        \"Define acidic hydrothermal conditions, residence time and transfer losses, demonstrating hydrolysis faster than decomposition before final cooling and buffering.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_001\": 0.91,\n        \"rxn_005\": 0.79,\n        \"rxn_007\": 0.88,\n        \"rxn_009\": 0.98\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.86,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.9,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.6,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.8,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.79,\n      \"feedback\": \"Alkaline nitrile hydration and amide hydrolysis are legitimate transformations, and the final protonation is correct. Their useful rates and selectivity in the proposed hydrothermal fluid are not e